window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-12-1", "source": "2023年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"As is known to all, gaining a sound knowledge of the basics is of vital importance for students to master an academic subject.”You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words (not including the sentence given).", "sample": "As is known to all, gaining a sound knowledge of the basics is of vital importance for students to master an\nacademic subject. Just as a towering building cannot stand firm without a solid foundation, no student can\nreach academic heights unless the fundamentals are first laid down. The basics are not the trivial part of\nlearning; they are the very ground on which deeper understanding is built.\nIn my own experience, this truth was made vivid when I began studying programming. At first I was eager to\nwrite fancy applications and skipped the elementary concepts of variables and loops. As a result, whenever a\nbug appeared, I had no idea where to look, and my code collapsed completely. It was only after I went back\nand mastered these basics that I could finally trace errors and write reliable programs. This lesson taught me\nthat shortcuts around the fundamentals lead nowhere.\nIt follows that students should never underestimate the value of the basics. Rather than rushing toward\ncomplicated tasks, we ought to devote patient effort to consolidating the fundamentals first. Only by standing\non such firm ground can we climb steadily toward true mastery of any subject."}, "translation": {"prompt": "在中国，随着老龄化社会的到来，养老受到普遍关注。人们谈论最多的是应当采取什么样的养老模式。多数人认为，养老模式需要多元化。可以通过政府引导和社会参与，建立更多更好的养老服务机构，改进社区服务中心，鼓励居家自助养老，还可以推行家庭养老与社会养老相结合的模式。随着政府和社会对养老服务事业投入的持续增加，养老设施将不断升级，服务质量逐步改进，老年人的生活将会更加方便舒适、健康快乐。", "sample": "In China, with the arrival of an aging society, elderly care has drawn widespread attention. What people talk about most is what model of elderly care should be adopted. Most people believe that the elderly-care model needs to be diversified. To achieve this, through governmental guidance and social participation, we can build more and better elderly-care institutions, improve community service centers, encourage self-supported home-based elderly care, and promote a combination of family-based and society-based elderly care. With the continuous increase in government and social investment in elderly- care services, elderly-care facilities will be steadily upgraded, service quality will gradually improve, and the elderly will live a more convenient, comfortable, healthy and happy life."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2023-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The desert is deceiving. At first glance it looks lifeless, barren, and bone-dry. For most passersby humming through the Mojave on their way to try their luck in Las Vegas or heading towards the Grand Canyon, it's just a  __26__  stretch of land with some mountains in the distance and more  __27__  to be a setting for a movie that takes place on Mars. The desert, however, is  __28__  with life, mystery, and magic.\nThe Mojave desert sees less than two inches of rain a year, and like most deserts, is a land of  __29__  . Temperature fluctuations vary from freezing to  __30__  hot, not only between seasons, but even within the same day.\nAt the heart of this vast, 25,000-square-mile desert is Mojave National Preserve. The folks managing and working there wear the wide-brimmed(宽边的) hats that have become  __31__  with places like Yosemite. The preserve is rich with history, culture, biodiversity, and endless opportunities to  __32__  your interest, especially for anyone who loves the outdoors. For a photographer, it requires patience and  __33__  a few spare tires to maneuver the network of unpaved \"roads\", but the rewards are plenty.\nPhotographing here requires a good,  __34__  pair of shoes and a lot of preparation. Plenty of water, spare tires, and a full tank of gas are needed to explore this unit of the national park system, which is the third largest in the country.\nLike all deserts, though, if you are patient, you will be rewarded, as they often reveal their secrets slowly. During my last outing, I was surprised to see how much of the area was shaped by ancient volcanoes and geological forces, much of which remains today, giving the area a  __35__  feel and painting a colorful background for great photographs.", "matchIntro": "African countries must get smarter with their agriculture", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "On the hills of central Kenya, almost lime-green with the sparkle of tea bushes in the sunlight, farmers know all about climate change. \"The rainy season is no longer predictable,\" says one. \"When it is supposed to rain it doesn't, then it all comes at once.\" Climate change is an issue that will affect everyone on the planet. For Africans its consequences will be particularly bitter: whereas other regions were able to grow rich by burning coal and oil, Africa will pay much of the human price without having enjoyed the benefits. \"Africa only represents 2% of global greenhouse-gas emissions but it is the continent that is expected to suffer the most from climate impacts,\" says Mafalda Duarte, who runs the World Bank's $8bn Climate Investment Funds."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Although there are huge uncertainties as to the precise impacts of climate change, enough is known to say that global warming represents one of the main threats to Africa's prosperity. Parts of the continent are already warming much more quickly than the average: temperatures in southern Africa have increased by about twice the global rate over the past 50 years. Even if the world were to cut emissions enough to keep global warming below 1.5℃, heat-waves would intensify in Africa and diseases would spread to areas not currently affected. Farming would also be hit hard. About 40% of the land now used to grow maize(玉米) would no longer be suitable for it. Overall, it is estimated that maize yields would fall by 18-22%."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Africa is particularly vulnerable, in part because it is already struggling to feed itself and it will have to vastly increase yields and productivity if it is to put food on the plates of a fast-growing population, even without climate change. The UN's Food and Agriculture Organisation reckons that by 2050 global food production would have to rise by about 70% over its level of 2009 to meet demand from a population that is growing in numbers and appetite. Much of this new demand will be in Africa. Yet the continent already imports about $50bn-worth of food a year and that figure is expected to more than double over the next five years. Self-sufficiency is not Africa's goal, but the fact that it spends more money importing food than it does buying capital goods suggests it has room for improvement."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Finding out why is not hard. Most farms are tiny, ploughed by hand and reliant on rain. More than half of Africa's people make their living from farming. Although its total harvest has climbed over the past few decades, this is mainly because there are more people farming more land. But in many places there is no spare land to farm. Plots in Rwanda are so small that you could fit 250 of them onto the average American farm. And although output per worker has improved by more than half over the past 30 years in Africa, that is still far behind the 2.5 times improvement in Asia. Yields of maize are generally less than two tonnes per hectare, a fifth the level in America."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The low productivity of African farmers is reflected in national economic statistics—despite absorbing so much labour, farming generates just 15% of GDP. \"They can't even feed their families,\" says Jennifer Blanke, a vice-president of the African Development Bank in charge of agriculture. \"Farm productivity hasn't improved in many parts of Africa for 100 years.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "One reason is that in the first few decades of independence, many African governments neglected farming as they focused on industrialising their economies. Others damaged it by pushing down the prices that state monopolies paid for their crops in order to subsidise workers in cities with cheap food. Ghana taxed cocoa(可可粉) exports so heavily that production collapsed by half between the 1960s and 1980s, despite a jump in the global price of cocoa. Yet over the past two decades or so governments and donors have begun to look again at farming as a way of providing jobs for the 13 million young people entering the workforce each year. Much of the focus has been on getting small farmers to use fertiliser and, more important, better seeds. The results can be impressive. Improved varieties of sorghum(高梁), for instance, can produce a crop that is 40% larger than the usual variety. Infrastructure is important. A World Bank irrigation project in Ethiopia helped farmers increase their potato harvest from about 8 tonnes per hectare to 35 tonnes."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Better techniques help, too. Small coffee farmers in Kenya are able to increase their incomes by 40% by following a few simple guidelines on caring for their bushes, such as trimming all but three of their stems. Many of their neighbours do not follow the advice, because it seems counter-intuitive. More stems ought to lead to more coffee beans, they say. Yet after seeing those following the advice get bigger harvests for a season or two, many others start doing the same."}, {"label": "H", "text": "One way of spreading knowledge is to link farms to big buyers of their harvests. When Diageo, a British drinks giant, built a brewery in western Kenya, it wanted to use local crops to make a beer cheap enough to compete with illicit home brew. It organised farmers into groups, improved supply chains for them to get seeds and fertiliser and then agreed to buy their grain. It now provides a market to about 17,000 farmers. Across the region it has doubled its use of local raw material to about 80% over five years, says John O'Keeffe, who runs its Africa business."}, {"label": "I", "text": "An even more important change is the move from traditional farming to building businesses that can profitably bring technology and investment to small farmers. Taita Ngetich, a young Kenyan, was studying engineering when he wanted to earn a little money on the side. He scraped together 20,000 Kenyan shillings (about $200) to plant tomatoes. Everything went wrong. The crop was attacked by pests. \"Then there was a massive flood that swallowed all our capital,\" he says. Mr Ngetich persevered by looking into buying a greenhouse to protect his plants from bugs and rain. The cheapest ones cost more than $2,500 each, so he designed his own for half the price. Soon neighbouring farmers started placing orders with him, and now his firm, Illuminum Greenhouses, has sold more than 1,400 greenhouses that provide livelihoods to about 6,000 people. The business does not stop there; he also supplies fertiliser, high-quality seedlings and smart sensors that increase yields."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Illuminum's success shows how technology can help even small farms become more productive. Because such a large share of Africa's population earns a living from agriculture, even small improvements in productivity can lift the incomes of millions of people. But over the longer run small-scale farming can go only so far, especially in the face of climate change and population pressure."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"If we really want to lift people out of poverty we have to finance projects that will get them an income of at least $100 a month so that they can pay for health care and education,\" says Mr Ngetich. \"Projects that give them an extra $2 a month from growing beans or maize aren't going to get them there.\" Getting those big jumps will need better jobs in factories and cities."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "One of the great successes of the Republican Party in recent decades is the relentless propagation of a simple formula for economic growth: tax cuts.\nThe formula doesn't work, but that has not affected its popularity. And while the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics, it lacks for obvious rivals.\nDemocratic politicians have tended to campaign on helping people left behind by economic growth. When Democrats do talk about encouraging economic growth, they often sound like Republicans.\nThis is not just a political problem for Democrats; it is an economic problem for the United States. The nation needs a better story about the drivers of economic growth. The painful lessons of recent decades point to a promising candidate: higher wages.\nRaising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic policymakers. We'd all be better off paying less attention to quarterly updates on the growth of the nation's gross domestic product (GDP) and focusing instead on the growth of workers' paychecks.\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness. The argument here is that higher wages can fuel the engine of economic growth.\nPerhaps the most famous illustration of the benefits is the story of Henry Ford's decision in 1914 to pay $5 a day to workers on his Model T assembly lines. He did it to increase production—he was paying a premium to maintain a reliable workforce. The unexpected benefit was that Ford's factory workers became Ford customers, too.\nThe same logic still holds: Consumption drives the American economy, and workers who are paid more can spend more.\nMainstream economists insist that it is impossible to order up a sustainable increase in wages because compensation levels reflect the unerring judgment of market forces.\nThe conventional wisdom held that productivity growth was the only route to higher wages. Through that lens, efforts to negotiate higher wages were counterproductive. Minimum-wage laws would raise unemployment because there was only so much money in the wage pool, and if some people got more, others would get none.\nIt was in the context of this worldview that it became popular to argue that tax cuts would drive prosperity. Rich people would invest, productivity would increase, wages would rise.\nIn the real world, things are more complicated. Wages are influenced by a tug of war between employers and workers, and employers have been winning. One clear piece of evidence is the widening gap between productivity growth and wage growth since roughly 1970. Productivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind.\nA focus on wage growth would provide an antidote(矫正方法) to the attractive simplicity of the belief in the magical power of tax cuts.", "47": "One of the great successes of the Republican Party in recent decades is the relentless propagation of a simple formula for economic growth: tax cuts.\nThe formula doesn't work, but that has not affected its popularity. And while the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics, it lacks for obvious rivals.\nDemocratic politicians have tended to campaign on helping people left behind by economic growth. When Democrats do talk about encouraging economic growth, they often sound like Republicans.\nThis is not just a political problem for Democrats; it is an economic problem for the United States. The nation needs a better story about the drivers of economic growth. The painful lessons of recent decades point to a promising candidate: higher wages.\nRaising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic policymakers. We'd all be better off paying less attention to quarterly updates on the growth of the nation's gross domestic product (GDP) and focusing instead on the growth of workers' paychecks.\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness. The argument here is that higher wages can fuel the engine of economic growth.\nPerhaps the most famous illustration of the benefits is the story of Henry Ford's decision in 1914 to pay $5 a day to workers on his Model T assembly lines. He did it to increase production—he was paying a premium to maintain a reliable workforce. The unexpected benefit was that Ford's factory workers became Ford customers, too.\nThe same logic still holds: Consumption drives the American economy, and workers who are paid more can spend more.\nMainstream economists insist that it is impossible to order up a sustainable increase in wages because compensation levels reflect the unerring judgment of market forces.\nThe conventional wisdom held that productivity growth was the only route to higher wages. Through that lens, efforts to negotiate higher wages were counterproductive. Minimum-wage laws would raise unemployment because there was only so much money in the wage pool, and if some people got more, others would get none.\nIt was in the context of this worldview that it became popular to argue that tax cuts would drive prosperity. Rich people would invest, productivity would increase, wages would rise.\nIn the real world, things are more complicated. Wages are influenced by a tug of war between employers and workers, and employers have been winning. One clear piece of evidence is the widening gap between productivity growth and wage growth since roughly 1970. Productivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind.\nA focus on wage growth would provide an antidote(矫正方法) to the attractive simplicity of the belief in the magical power of tax cuts.", "48": "One of the great successes of the Republican Party in recent decades is the relentless propagation of a simple formula for economic growth: tax cuts.\nThe formula doesn't work, but that has not affected its popularity. And while the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics, it lacks for obvious rivals.\nDemocratic politicians have tended to campaign on helping people left behind by economic growth. When Democrats do talk about encouraging economic growth, they often sound like Republicans.\nThis is not just a political problem for Democrats; it is an economic problem for the United States. The nation needs a better story about the drivers of economic growth. The painful lessons of recent decades point to a promising candidate: higher wages.\nRaising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic policymakers. We'd all be better off paying less attention to quarterly updates on the growth of the nation's gross domestic product (GDP) and focusing instead on the growth of workers' paychecks.\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness. The argument here is that higher wages can fuel the engine of economic growth.\nPerhaps the most famous illustration of the benefits is the story of Henry Ford's decision in 1914 to pay $5 a day to workers on his Model T assembly lines. He did it to increase production—he was paying a premium to maintain a reliable workforce. The unexpected benefit was that Ford's factory workers became Ford customers, too.\nThe same logic still holds: Consumption drives the American economy, and workers who are paid more can spend more.\nMainstream economists insist that it is impossible to order up a sustainable increase in wages because compensation levels reflect the unerring judgment of market forces.\nThe conventional wisdom held that productivity growth was the only route to higher wages. Through that lens, efforts to negotiate higher wages were counterproductive. Minimum-wage laws would raise unemployment because there was only so much money in the wage pool, and if some people got more, others would get none.\nIt was in the context of this worldview that it became popular to argue that tax cuts would drive prosperity. Rich people would invest, productivity would increase, wages would rise.\nIn the real world, things are more complicated. Wages are influenced by a tug of war between employers and workers, and employers have been winning. One clear piece of evidence is the widening gap between productivity growth and wage growth since roughly 1970. Productivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind.\nA focus on wage growth would provide an antidote(矫正方法) to the attractive simplicity of the belief in the magical power of tax cuts.", "49": "One of the great successes of the Republican Party in recent decades is the relentless propagation of a simple formula for economic growth: tax cuts.\nThe formula doesn't work, but that has not affected its popularity. And while the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics, it lacks for obvious rivals.\nDemocratic politicians have tended to campaign on helping people left behind by economic growth. When Democrats do talk about encouraging economic growth, they often sound like Republicans.\nThis is not just a political problem for Democrats; it is an economic problem for the United States. The nation needs a better story about the drivers of economic growth. The painful lessons of recent decades point to a promising candidate: higher wages.\nRaising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic policymakers. We'd all be better off paying less attention to quarterly updates on the growth of the nation's gross domestic product (GDP) and focusing instead on the growth of workers' paychecks.\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness. The argument here is that higher wages can fuel the engine of economic growth.\nPerhaps the most famous illustration of the benefits is the story of Henry Ford's decision in 1914 to pay $5 a day to workers on his Model T assembly lines. He did it to increase production—he was paying a premium to maintain a reliable workforce. The unexpected benefit was that Ford's factory workers became Ford customers, too.\nThe same logic still holds: Consumption drives the American economy, and workers who are paid more can spend more.\nMainstream economists insist that it is impossible to order up a sustainable increase in wages because compensation levels reflect the unerring judgment of market forces.\nThe conventional wisdom held that productivity growth was the only route to higher wages. Through that lens, efforts to negotiate higher wages were counterproductive. Minimum-wage laws would raise unemployment because there was only so much money in the wage pool, and if some people got more, others would get none.\nIt was in the context of this worldview that it became popular to argue that tax cuts would drive prosperity. Rich people would invest, productivity would increase, wages would rise.\nIn the real world, things are more complicated. Wages are influenced by a tug of war between employers and workers, and employers have been winning. One clear piece of evidence is the widening gap between productivity growth and wage growth since roughly 1970. Productivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind.\nA focus on wage growth would provide an antidote(矫正方法) to the attractive simplicity of the belief in the magical power of tax cuts.", "50": "One of the great successes of the Republican Party in recent decades is the relentless propagation of a simple formula for economic growth: tax cuts.\nThe formula doesn't work, but that has not affected its popularity. And while the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics, it lacks for obvious rivals.\nDemocratic politicians have tended to campaign on helping people left behind by economic growth. When Democrats do talk about encouraging economic growth, they often sound like Republicans.\nThis is not just a political problem for Democrats; it is an economic problem for the United States. The nation needs a better story about the drivers of economic growth. The painful lessons of recent decades point to a promising candidate: higher wages.\nRaising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic policymakers. We'd all be better off paying less attention to quarterly updates on the growth of the nation's gross domestic product (GDP) and focusing instead on the growth of workers' paychecks.\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness. The argument here is that higher wages can fuel the engine of economic growth.\nPerhaps the most famous illustration of the benefits is the story of Henry Ford's decision in 1914 to pay $5 a day to workers on his Model T assembly lines. He did it to increase production—he was paying a premium to maintain a reliable workforce. The unexpected benefit was that Ford's factory workers became Ford customers, too.\nThe same logic still holds: Consumption drives the American economy, and workers who are paid more can spend more.\nMainstream economists insist that it is impossible to order up a sustainable increase in wages because compensation levels reflect the unerring judgment of market forces.\nThe conventional wisdom held that productivity growth was the only route to higher wages. Through that lens, efforts to negotiate higher wages were counterproductive. Minimum-wage laws would raise unemployment because there was only so much money in the wage pool, and if some people got more, others would get none.\nIt was in the context of this worldview that it became popular to argue that tax cuts would drive prosperity. Rich people would invest, productivity would increase, wages would rise.\nIn the real world, things are more complicated. Wages are influenced by a tug of war between employers and workers, and employers have been winning. One clear piece of evidence is the widening gap between productivity growth and wage growth since roughly 1970. Productivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind.\nA focus on wage growth would provide an antidote(矫正方法) to the attractive simplicity of the belief in the magical power of tax cuts.", "51": "Journal editors decide what gets published and what doesn't, affecting the careers of other academics and influencing the direction that a field takes. You'd hope, then, that journals would do everything they can to establish a diverse editorial board, reflecting a variety of voices, experiences, and identities.\nUnfortunately a new study in Nature Neuroscience makes for disheartening reading. The team finds that the majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are male and based in the United States: a situation that may be amplifying existing gender inequalities in the field and influencing the kind of research that gets published.\nMen were found to account for 60% of the editors of psychology journals. There were significantly more male than female editors at each level of seniority, and men made up the majority of editors in over three quarters of the journals. Crucially, the proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion of women psychology researchers.\nThe differences were even starker in the neuroscience journals: 70% of editors were male, and men held the majority of editorial positions in 88% of journals. In this case, the proportion of female editors was not significantly lower than the proportion of female researchers working in neuroscience—a finding that reveals enduring gender disparities in the field more broadly.\nBased on their results, the team concludes that \"the ideas, values and decision-making biases of men are overrepresented in the editorial positions of the most recognized academic journals in psychology and neuroscience.\"\nGender inequality in science is often attributed to the fact that senior academics are more likely to be male, because historically science was male-dominated: it's argued that as time goes on and more women rise to senior roles, the field will become more equal. Yet this study showed that even the junior roles in psychology journals tended to be held disproportionately by men, despite the fact that there are actually more female than male junior psychology faculty.\nThis implies that a lack of female academics is not the problem. Instead, there are structural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science. Women receive lower salaries and face greater childcare demands, for instance, which can result in fewer publications and grants—the kinds of things that journals look for when deciding who to appoint. Rather than simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition, we should be actively breaking down these existing barriers.\nA lack of diversity among journal editors also likely contributes to psychology's WEIRD problem. If journal editors are largely men from the United States, then they will probably place higher value on papers that are relevant to Western, male populations, whether consciously or not.", "52": "Journal editors decide what gets published and what doesn't, affecting the careers of other academics and influencing the direction that a field takes. You'd hope, then, that journals would do everything they can to establish a diverse editorial board, reflecting a variety of voices, experiences, and identities.\nUnfortunately a new study in Nature Neuroscience makes for disheartening reading. The team finds that the majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are male and based in the United States: a situation that may be amplifying existing gender inequalities in the field and influencing the kind of research that gets published.\nMen were found to account for 60% of the editors of psychology journals. There were significantly more male than female editors at each level of seniority, and men made up the majority of editors in over three quarters of the journals. Crucially, the proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion of women psychology researchers.\nThe differences were even starker in the neuroscience journals: 70% of editors were male, and men held the majority of editorial positions in 88% of journals. In this case, the proportion of female editors was not significantly lower than the proportion of female researchers working in neuroscience—a finding that reveals enduring gender disparities in the field more broadly.\nBased on their results, the team concludes that \"the ideas, values and decision-making biases of men are overrepresented in the editorial positions of the most recognized academic journals in psychology and neuroscience.\"\nGender inequality in science is often attributed to the fact that senior academics are more likely to be male, because historically science was male-dominated: it's argued that as time goes on and more women rise to senior roles, the field will become more equal. Yet this study showed that even the junior roles in psychology journals tended to be held disproportionately by men, despite the fact that there are actually more female than male junior psychology faculty.\nThis implies that a lack of female academics is not the problem. Instead, there are structural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science. Women receive lower salaries and face greater childcare demands, for instance, which can result in fewer publications and grants—the kinds of things that journals look for when deciding who to appoint. Rather than simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition, we should be actively breaking down these existing barriers.\nA lack of diversity among journal editors also likely contributes to psychology's WEIRD problem. If journal editors are largely men from the United States, then they will probably place higher value on papers that are relevant to Western, male populations, whether consciously or not.", "53": "Journal editors decide what gets published and what doesn't, affecting the careers of other academics and influencing the direction that a field takes. You'd hope, then, that journals would do everything they can to establish a diverse editorial board, reflecting a variety of voices, experiences, and identities.\nUnfortunately a new study in Nature Neuroscience makes for disheartening reading. The team finds that the majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are male and based in the United States: a situation that may be amplifying existing gender inequalities in the field and influencing the kind of research that gets published.\nMen were found to account for 60% of the editors of psychology journals. There were significantly more male than female editors at each level of seniority, and men made up the majority of editors in over three quarters of the journals. Crucially, the proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion of women psychology researchers.\nThe differences were even starker in the neuroscience journals: 70% of editors were male, and men held the majority of editorial positions in 88% of journals. In this case, the proportion of female editors was not significantly lower than the proportion of female researchers working in neuroscience—a finding that reveals enduring gender disparities in the field more broadly.\nBased on their results, the team concludes that \"the ideas, values and decision-making biases of men are overrepresented in the editorial positions of the most recognized academic journals in psychology and neuroscience.\"\nGender inequality in science is often attributed to the fact that senior academics are more likely to be male, because historically science was male-dominated: it's argued that as time goes on and more women rise to senior roles, the field will become more equal. Yet this study showed that even the junior roles in psychology journals tended to be held disproportionately by men, despite the fact that there are actually more female than male junior psychology faculty.\nThis implies that a lack of female academics is not the problem. Instead, there are structural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science. Women receive lower salaries and face greater childcare demands, for instance, which can result in fewer publications and grants—the kinds of things that journals look for when deciding who to appoint. Rather than simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition, we should be actively breaking down these existing barriers.\nA lack of diversity among journal editors also likely contributes to psychology's WEIRD problem. If journal editors are largely men from the United States, then they will probably place higher value on papers that are relevant to Western, male populations, whether consciously or not.", "54": "Journal editors decide what gets published and what doesn't, affecting the careers of other academics and influencing the direction that a field takes. You'd hope, then, that journals would do everything they can to establish a diverse editorial board, reflecting a variety of voices, experiences, and identities.\nUnfortunately a new study in Nature Neuroscience makes for disheartening reading. The team finds that the majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are male and based in the United States: a situation that may be amplifying existing gender inequalities in the field and influencing the kind of research that gets published.\nMen were found to account for 60% of the editors of psychology journals. There were significantly more male than female editors at each level of seniority, and men made up the majority of editors in over three quarters of the journals. Crucially, the proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion of women psychology researchers.\nThe differences were even starker in the neuroscience journals: 70% of editors were male, and men held the majority of editorial positions in 88% of journals. In this case, the proportion of female editors was not significantly lower than the proportion of female researchers working in neuroscience—a finding that reveals enduring gender disparities in the field more broadly.\nBased on their results, the team concludes that \"the ideas, values and decision-making biases of men are overrepresented in the editorial positions of the most recognized academic journals in psychology and neuroscience.\"\nGender inequality in science is often attributed to the fact that senior academics are more likely to be male, because historically science was male-dominated: it's argued that as time goes on and more women rise to senior roles, the field will become more equal. Yet this study showed that even the junior roles in psychology journals tended to be held disproportionately by men, despite the fact that there are actually more female than male junior psychology faculty.\nThis implies that a lack of female academics is not the problem. Instead, there are structural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science. Women receive lower salaries and face greater childcare demands, for instance, which can result in fewer publications and grants—the kinds of things that journals look for when deciding who to appoint. Rather than simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition, we should be actively breaking down these existing barriers.\nA lack of diversity among journal editors also likely contributes to psychology's WEIRD problem. If journal editors are largely men from the United States, then they will probably place higher value on papers that are relevant to Western, male populations, whether consciously or not.", "55": "Journal editors decide what gets published and what doesn't, affecting the careers of other academics and influencing the direction that a field takes. You'd hope, then, that journals would do everything they can to establish a diverse editorial board, reflecting a variety of voices, experiences, and identities.\nUnfortunately a new study in Nature Neuroscience makes for disheartening reading. The team finds that the majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are male and based in the United States: a situation that may be amplifying existing gender inequalities in the field and influencing the kind of research that gets published.\nMen were found to account for 60% of the editors of psychology journals. There were significantly more male than female editors at each level of seniority, and men made up the majority of editors in over three quarters of the journals. Crucially, the proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion of women psychology researchers.\nThe differences were even starker in the neuroscience journals: 70% of editors were male, and men held the majority of editorial positions in 88% of journals. In this case, the proportion of female editors was not significantly lower than the proportion of female researchers working in neuroscience—a finding that reveals enduring gender disparities in the field more broadly.\nBased on their results, the team concludes that \"the ideas, values and decision-making biases of men are overrepresented in the editorial positions of the most recognized academic journals in psychology and neuroscience.\"\nGender inequality in science is often attributed to the fact that senior academics are more likely to be male, because historically science was male-dominated: it's argued that as time goes on and more women rise to senior roles, the field will become more equal. Yet this study showed that even the junior roles in psychology journals tended to be held disproportionately by men, despite the fact that there are actually more female than male junior psychology faculty.\nThis implies that a lack of female academics is not the problem. Instead, there are structural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science. Women receive lower salaries and face greater childcare demands, for instance, which can result in fewer publications and grants—the kinds of things that journals look for when deciding who to appoint. Rather than simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition, we should be actively breaking down these existing barriers.\nA lack of diversity among journal editors also likely contributes to psychology's WEIRD problem. If journal editors are largely men from the United States, then they will probably place higher value on papers that are relevant to Western, male populations, whether consciously or not."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the man from his test results?", "options": ["A) It is clear that he is expected to enjoy a healthy life.", "B) There is nothing wrong with his digestive system.", "C) There is some indication of an issue with his blood circulation.", "D) He doesn't know he has long been suffering from poor health."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） There is nothing wrong with his digestive system.\n【定位】The blood test came back clear. There is no indication of any digestive issues.\n血液化验结果是清白的（没问题）。没有任何消化系统问题的迹象。\n【信号】医生宣布结果的直接陈述句。医生一坐下就直接通报结论 The blood test came back clear\n并补一句 There is no indication of any digestive issues——这种“医生直接公布检查结\n果”的陈述就是答案信号，不用等转折。\n【替换】no indication of any digestive issues ↔ nothing wrong with his digestive system\n“没有消化问题迹象”=“消化系统没毛病”\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n录音查的是 blood test 与 digestive issues，an issue with his blood circulation 借用了\n“血液 blood”的耳熟感，却把对象偷换成 blood circulation（血液循环），录音从未说循环\n有问题，且结果是 clear（清白），与“有问题”正好相反。\n借用原文：The blood test came back clear\nA) 过度推断\n录音只说某项检查没问题，It is clear that he is expected to enjoy a healthy life 却推到\n“可望享有健康人生”——录音里他正feel poorly、被诊断有压力，谈不上健康一生，是无中\n生有的拔高。\nD) 答非所问\nHe doesn't know he has long been suffering from poor health 套用了后文“压力潜伏二\n十年而不自知”的影子，但本题问的是 test results（化验结果），结果恰恰是“清白”，并未\n说明他长期体弱，错位安插。\n借用原文：You could have been suffering from stress for 20 years without knowing it", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the woman think is a common phenomenon among many people?", "options": ["A) Mistaking symptoms of illness for stress.", "B) Complaining they are being overworked.", "C) Being unaware of the stress they are under.", "D) Suffering from illness without recognising it."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Being unaware of the stress they are under.\n【定位】What you have just described is a common sentiment. Many people who suffer from stress\nfail to recognise it.\n你刚才描述的是一种很常见的心态。许多承受压力的人都意识不到自己有压力。\n【信号】含 common / Many people 的概括句。女方用 a common sentiment 与 Many people\nwho suffer from stress fail to recognise it 对“很多人”下普遍性判断——出现 common、\nMany people 这类总括词，就是“普遍现象”题的答案信号。\n【替换】Many people who suffer from stress fail to recognise it ↔\nBeing unaware of the stress they are under\n“承压却没认出”=“没意识到自己身处压力”\n【排除】D) 偷换概念\nSuffering from illness without recognising it 与答案句框架一致（都是“X 而不自知”），\n但 X 被换成 illness；录音说的是 suffer from stress fail to recognise it——没认出的是压\n力不是疾病，差的正是那个核心变量。\n借用原文：Many people who suffer from stress fail to recognise it\nA) 与原文相反\nMistaking symptoms of illness for stress 是“把病当成压力”，而录音是“有压力却没认出\n（把压力当别的）”，方向恰好反过来；男方原话是 I've never felt stress，是不承认压力，\n不是错把病当压力。\nB) 原词陷阱\nComplaining they are being overworked 蹭了录音 overwork and stress 的\noverwork，但女方说的是很多人“没意识到压力”，恰恰不是“抱怨过劳”；男方甚至说 other\npeople complain，他自己不抱怨，与“很多人抱怨”相反。\n借用原文：It's probably due to overwork and stress", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman say she will do for the man?", "options": ["A) Prescribe some medication for him.", "B) Give him another physical check-up.", "C) Explain to him the common consequence of stress.", "D) Buy some sleeping pills for him from the drugstore."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Prescribe some medication for him.\n【定位】I'm going to prescribe some special sleeping pills for you.\n我要给你开一些特制的安眠药。\n【信号】I'm going to + 动词的将来表态句。女方用 I'm going to prescribe some special\nsleeping pills for you 主动声明下一步动作——I'm going to 这类将来式表态，就是“她将\n做什么”题的答案信号。\n【替换】prescribe some special sleeping pills ↔ Prescribe some medication for him\n“开安眠药”=“开药（medication）”\n【排除】D) 原词陷阱\nBuy some sleeping pills for him from the drugstore 盗用了原句的 sleeping pills，却把\n动作从“医生开处方 prescribe”改成“去药店买 buy from the drugstore”；录音里医生是开\n方，不是替他去药店买药。\n借用原文：I'm going to prescribe some special sleeping pills for you\nC) 答非所问\nExplain to him the common consequence of stress 蹭了录音 Those are common\nconsequences of stress，但那是医生在解释失眠成因，不是回答“她将为他做什么”这一动\n作；本题要的是她接下来要做的事，是开药。\n借用原文：Those are common consequences of stress\nB) 未提及\nGive him another physical check-up 录音从未提及再做体检——医生是看着已有的 test\nresults 直接开药，并约一个月后复诊，没有安排再次体检。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about the industry he is engaged in?", "options": ["A) It calls for responsible management.", "B) It proves to be quite profitable.", "C) It is remarkably promising.", "D) It is full of competition."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is full of competition.\n【定位】I'm a restaurant manager and this industry is very competitive.\n我是个餐厅经理，这个行业竞争非常激烈。\n【信号】this industry is very + 形容词的直接评价句。男方自陈身份后用 this industry is very\ncompetitive 直接评价所在行业——this industry is very + 形容词的结构就是“行业评价”\n题的答案信号，落点在 competitive 这个词。\n【替换】this industry is very competitive ↔ It is full of competition\n“非常有竞争性”=“充满竞争”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt calls for responsible management 蹭了男方自报身份 I'm a restaurant manager 里的\nmanager/management，但他评价行业用的词是 competitive，从没说这行“需要负责任的\n管理”，是借身份词造的干扰。\n借用原文：I'm a restaurant manager\nB) 未提及\nIt proves to be quite profitable 谈盈利，但男方对行业的描述只有“竞争激烈”和“事务繁\n多”，从未提到赚钱与否，是无中生有。\nC) 未提及\nIt is remarkably promising 说行业前景可观，但录音里男方谈的是竞争和繁忙、并被建议\n放慢节奏，全无“前景大好”之意，属凭空添加的正面评价。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "Why did John Williams leave the sport of basketball at just 25?", "options": ["A) To avoid being in the limelight.", "B) To seek medical help for his injury.", "C) To pursue a less competitive career.", "D) To stay away from his hostile teammates."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To avoid being in the limelight.\n【定位】I loved being an athlete, but I didn't love being a celebrity. I was in the limelight when I was\nstill a high school student and went professional right after high school graduation, which was\na mistake. I was a shy kid and I wasn't ready for all the media attention.\n我热爱当运动员，但我不喜欢当名人。我还在念高中时就处在聚光灯下，高中一毕业就转入\n职业，这是个错误。我是个害羞的孩子，还没准备好应对这一切媒体关注。\n【信号】被问者应答 + 转折 but 见真意。主持人问他为何退役，被问者亲口回答的这句就是答案\n句；其中 but I didn\\'t love being a celebrity 的转折 but 之后才是真正的退役理由，前半\nI loved being an athlete 是铺垫干扰。\n【替换】I didn\\'t love being a celebrity ↔ To avoid being in the limelight.\n不爱当名人=避开聚光灯\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 借用了原文里 injury 一词，但那是主持人提出的传闻 you were having issues with your\nteammates or even an injury，被问者随即以 Not at all 明确否认，故因伤就医并非退役\n原因。\n借用原文：there was speculation that you were having issues with your teammates or even\nan injury\nC) 过度推断\n他确实想趁年轻转行，但原文动机是 basketball wasn\\'t a career with a lot of longevity\n（这行干不长），并非为追求「竞争更小」的职业，C 是无中生有地编了个理由。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 借用原文 teammates 一词，但 issues with your teammates 同样只是主持人的猜测，\n被 Not at all 一并否认，与队友不和并非退役原因。\n借用原文：you were having issues with your teammates", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say about basketball as a career for most players?", "options": ["A) It has ups and downs.", "B) It proves rewarding.", "C) It does not last long.", "D) It is not so profitable."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） It does not last long.\n【定位】I knew basketball wasn't a career with a lot of longevity for most players, so I wanted to\nchange careers while I was still young.\n我知道对大多数球员来说，篮球不是个能干很久的职业，所以我想趁年轻就转行。\n【信号】被问者亲口下的定性句。这句 basketball wasn\\'t a career with a lot of longevity for\nmost players 是男士对篮球职业寿命的直接判断，与题干 for most players 的限定完全对\n应，是答案句。\n【替换】wasn\\'t a career with a lot of longevity ↔ It does not last long.\n缺乏长久性=持续不久\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文只说这行干不长，从未提到 ups and downs（有起有落），A 是凭印象编出的评价，\n属无中生有。\nB) 答非所问\n他确实说热爱篮球、留恋球队，但这是个人情感，并非说篮球作为职业 proves rewarding\n（有回报）；B 借了正面语气却与「问职业属性」答非所问。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 借用了这段反复出现的 money 话题，但原文是 I\\'d actually earned a lot and saved\nmost of it，说明他挺赚钱，与 not so profitable（不赚钱）正好相反。\n借用原文：I\\'d actually earned a lot and saved most of it", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do we learn from the woman about John Williams 20 years later?", "options": ["A) He was a financial advisor.", "B) He suffered from poor health.", "C) He became a basketball coach.", "D) He was back in the news."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He was back in the news.\n【定位】Okay, that was 20 years ago, and you're back in the news. You've created a foundation that\nworks to get more kids playing team sports.\n好，那是二十年前的事了，如今你重回新闻视野。你创立了一个基金会，致力于让更多孩子\n参与团队运动。\n【信号】女士口中 20 years 时间锚句。由女士说出、带时间标志 that was 20 years ago, and\nyou\\'re back in the news，正对应题干的 woman 与 20 years later，是答案句。\n【替换】you\\'re back in the news ↔ He was back in the news.\n你重回新闻=他重回新闻\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用了原文 I had great financial advisers 里的 financial，但那说的是他当年请了理财\n顾问，而非他本人 20 年后成了理财顾问，时间与身份都被错置。\n借用原文：I had great financial advisers\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文谈 health 是指他后来研究 public health、关注 obesity，并非他自己 suffered from\npoor health；把「研究健康问题」误读成「他健康不佳」，属把内容安错对象。\n借用原文：I studied public health\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 借用反复出现的 basketball，但原文 20 年后他做的是创立基金会推动孩子运动，从未说\n他 became a basketball coach，教练身份系无中生有。\n借用原文：You\\'ve created a foundation that works to get more kids playing team sports", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What has the man spent the last two decades trying to do?", "options": ["A) Study issues of public health.", "B) Alleviate the obesity problem.", "C) Raise sufficient public funding.", "D) Train young basketball players."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Alleviate the obesity problem.\n【定位】I studied public health and learned about the seriousness of the obesity epidemic, particularly\namong kids and adolescents in poor communities. I've spent the last two decades trying to\nalleviate the problem.\n我研究了公共健康，了解到肥胖流行问题的严重性，尤其是在贫困社区的儿童和青少年中。\n过去二十年我一直在努力缓解这个问题。\n【信号】spent...trying 与题干同款行动句。原文 I\\'ve spent the last two decades trying to\nalleviate the problem 与题干 spent the last two decades trying to do 几乎逐词对应，\n是直接锁定答案的持续行动句。\n【替换】trying to alleviate the problem ↔ Alleviate the obesity problem.\n缓解这个问题=缓解肥胖问题\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用原文 I studied public health，但研究公共健康是他在大学里的学习，是认识问题的\n前提，而非他二十年来「致力做」的那件事；真正的长期目标是缓解肥胖。\n借用原文：I studied public health\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 借用原文 also have some public funding 里的 public funding，但那只是基金会的资\n金来源之一，原文并未说他二十年来一直在筹集足够的公共资金，属错置内容。\n借用原文：also have some public funding\nD) 偷换概念\nD 借用篮球相关词，原文确有 basketball teams 与让孩子打球，但那是缓解肥胖的手段，\n且对象是普通中小学生而非培养 young basketball players，目的被偷换。\n借用原文：support basketball teams for girls and boys in primary school", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "When did Kate Atkinson begin to write for women's magazines?", "options": ["A) When she started teaching at Edinburgh University in Scotland.", "B) While she was doing her doctoral studies on American Literature.", "C) After publishing her first novel Behind the Scenes at the Museum.", "D) After winning the 1986 Woman's Own Short Story Competition."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） After winning the 1986 Woman's Own Short Story Competition.\n【定位】She began writing for women's magazines after winning the 1986 Woman's Own Short\nStory Competition.\n她在赢得 1986 年《Woman's Own》短篇小说大赛之后,开始为女性杂志写作。\n【信号】after 时间标志词直接锁定时点。答案句里 after winning the 1986 Woman's Own Short\nStory Competition 的 after 是明确的时间定向词,把\"开始为女性杂志写作\"这一动作精确挂\n在\"获奖\"事件之后——听到\"writing for women's magazines\"紧跟 after,就锁定这一句。\n【替换】after winning the 1986 Woman's Own Short Story Competition ↔\nAfter winning the 1986 Woman's Own Short Story Competition.\n原文与选项近乎逐字同形,只需对住事件\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nWhen she started teaching at Edinburgh University in Scotland. 盗用了原文 she\ntaught at the university she graduated from 这一真实事件来诱选,但任教在前(1981 写\n短篇之前),并非\"为女性杂志写作\"的时点;且原文是\"母校\"而非 Edinburgh University,地点也\n被偷改。\n借用原文：Later, she taught at the university she graduated from\nB) 原词陷阱\nWhile she was doing her doctoral studies on American Literature. 借原文 she\nresearched a doctorate on American Literature 设套,读博发生在 1974 年毕业后、远早\n于 1986 年,并非为女性杂志写作的时间。\n借用原文：After graduating in 1974, she researched a doctorate on American Literature\nC) 原词陷阱\nAfter publishing her first novel Behind the Scenes at the Museum. 借原文 Her first\nnovel, Behind the Scenes at the Museum, won the 1995 UK Book of the Year award\n设套;出版在 1995 年、晚于为女性杂志写作的 1986 年,时序正好反了。\n借用原文：Her first novel, Behind the Scenes at the Museum, won the 1995 UK Book of the\nYear award", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What did Kate Atkinson's books touch on?", "options": ["A) The themes of love and loss.", "B) The code of human behaviour.", "C) The sins and flaws of eccentrics.", "D) The manners of fashionable circles."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The themes of love and loss.\n【定位】Whatever genre Atkinson writes in, her books touch on the themes of love and loss and how\nto carry on.\n无论写什么体裁,阿特金森的书都触及爱与失、以及如何继续生活下去这些主题。\n【信号】题干同词 touch on 领起主题,直给答案。答案句 her books touch on the themes of love\nand loss 用的动词与题干 touch on 完全同款,等于原文亲口把答案领出来——听到 \"her\nbooks touch on…\" 就锁这句,后面紧跟的主题即答案。\n【替换】the themes of love and loss ↔ The themes of love and loss.\n主题表述逐字对应\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nThe code of human behaviour. 取自原文后文 the complex and sophisticated code of\nbehaviour,但那是在解释\"风尚喜剧\"这一文类的定义,不是回答\"her books touch on 什么主\n题\"的内容,答非所问。\n借用原文：the complex and sophisticated code of behaviour current in fashionable circles\nof society\nC) 答非所问\nThe sins and flaws of eccentrics. 拼自原文 sometimes sinful, and generally flawed\neccentrics,但那句讲的是书里\"住着哪类人物形象\",而题目问的是 touch on 的\"主题\",混淆\n了\"登场人物\"与\"核心主题\"。\n借用原文：populated by odd, sometimes sinful, and generally flawed eccentrics\nD) 答非所问\nThe manners of fashionable circles. 取自原文 code of behaviour current in\nfashionable circles of society 与 comedies of manners,这些是解释\"风尚喜剧\"文类标签\n的措辞,而非她书的 touch on 主题。\n借用原文：comedies that represent the complex and sophisticated code of behaviour current in\nfashionable circles of society", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do we learn about the clever and deceitful characters in a comedy of manners?", "options": ["A) They are usually ignorant of complex human relations.", "B) They successfully imitate the manners of celebrities.", "C) They often get rewarded instead of being punished.", "D) They are generally looked down upon in society."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They often get rewarded instead of being punished.\n【定位】A comedy of manners tends to reward its clever and deceitful characters, rather than punish\ntheir bad deeds.\n风尚喜剧往往奖赏它那些聪明而狡诈的角色,而不是惩罚他们的恶行。\n【信号】reward…rather than punish 的对比定向句。答案句用 reward its clever and deceitful\ncharacters, rather than punish their bad deeds 这一\"奖赏 A、而非惩罚 B\"的对比结构直\n接交代结局——rather than 是关键定向词,把方向钉死在\"受赏\"一边;听到 reward…rather\nthan punish 就锁这句。\n【替换】reward its clever and deceitful characters, rather than punish their bad deeds ↔\nThey often get rewarded instead of being punished.\n主动→被动 + rather than=instead of 同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey are usually ignorant of complex human relations. 把原文 the complex and\nsophisticated code of behaviour 里的 complex 一词揪出来拼成\"对复杂人际无知\",但原\n文从未说这些角色\"无知\",纯属据片词臆造。\n借用原文：the complex and sophisticated code of behaviour\nB) 未提及\nThey successfully imitate the manners of celebrities. 借原文 manners 一词造势,但原文\n只字未提这些角色\"成功模仿名人\",imitate/celebrities 都无出处,属凭空添加。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey are generally looked down upon in society. 取了\"受罚/负面\"的方向,正好与原文\nreward…rather than punish 的\"受赏不受罚\"相反——原文明说是被奖赏,本项却说被社会\n看轻,极性反了。\n借用原文：rather than punish their bad deeds", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why does the speaker say adaptability is an important skill to exercise in the workplace?", "options": ["A) It is what members use to alleviate tension in a team.", "B) It is what employers are increasingly seeking after.", "C) It is conducive to getting over a recession.", "D) It is necessary for learning a new task."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is what employers are increasingly seeking after.\n【定位】Simply put, adaptability is a skill employers are increasingly looking for.\n简单说，适应力是一种雇主越来越看重的技能。\n【信号】Simply put 引出总论断。开篇自问 Why 之后紧跟 Simply put, adaptability is a skill\nemployers are increasingly looking for，Simply put 是抛出核心结论的信号词，这句就\n是对 Why 的直接回答。\n【替换】employers are increasingly looking for ↔ employers are increasingly seeking after\nlooking for 同义于 seeking after\n【排除】A) 未提及\nalleviate tension in a team 在录音里根本没出现，适应力被说成雇主看重的技能而非用来\n化解团队紧张的工具，属凭空捏造。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确念了 economy might enter a recession，但那是举例说明环境会变，并非说适\n应力有助于度过衰退；C 盗用 recession 制造听到就想选的诱惑，答非所问。\n借用原文：economy might enter a recession\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 learning a new task 是说学新任务时生产力会上升，并非说适应力对学新任务必不\n可少；D 把这个原词搬来当重要性的理由，错位。\n借用原文：When you spend time learning a new task rather than resisting it, your productivity\ngoes up.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does adaptability enable us to do according to strategy consultant Dorie Clark?", "options": ["A) Make better choices.", "B) Follow innovative ideas.", "C) Achieve recognition duly.", "D) Accumulate work experience."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Make better choices.\n【定位】That enables you to make more accurate, informed and effective choices.\n这使你能够做出更准确、更明智、更有效的选择。\n【信号】enables you to 的因果落点。Dorie Clark 一番话的收尾句 That enables you to make\nmore accurate, informed and effective choices，enables you to 正是题干 enable us\nto do 的对应信号，结论就在这句。\n【替换】make more accurate, informed and effective choices ↔ Make better choices.\n三个褒义形容词概括为 better\n【排除】B) 未提及\nFollow innovative ideas. 在 Dorie Clark 的话里没有依据，她讲的是认清变化、做出选\n择，未提跟随创新想法。\nC) 未提及\nAchieve recognition duly. 谈获得认可，录音从未涉及，是凭空添加的职场正能量诱饵。\nD) 答非所问\n录音说环境会变、要据实采取行动，并未提积累经验；Accumulate work experience. 借职\n场话题诱你联想，但答案句讲的是做选择，并非攒经验。\n借用原文：you can take appropriate action based on what is, rather than how you wish the world\nwould be", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do we learn about today's work culture from the passage?", "options": ["A) Workers show more emotional intelligence.", "B) Workers use brains more than muscles.", "C) People usually work flexible hours.", "D) People often work in teams."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） People often work in teams.\n【定位】Today's work culture and management style is often based on teamwork, rather than a\nrigid hierarchy.\n如今的工作文化和管理方式往往建立在团队合作之上，而非僵化的等级制度。\n【信号】Today's work culture 直接点题。第二段开头 Today's work culture and management\nstyle is often based on teamwork 与题干用词几乎一致，是直接点题句，答案就在 based\non teamwork。\n【替换】based on teamwork ↔ People often work in teams.\n名词 teamwork 转为动作 work in teams\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确念了 emotional intelligence，但那是说头脑风暴需要情商，并非说工作者展现出更\n多情商；A 把这个原词搬来当工作文化特征，安错了对象。\n借用原文：Brainstorming, which requires creativity, flexibility, and emotional intelligence\nB) 未提及\nWorkers use brains more than muscles. 这种脑力对体力的对比录音从没说，是凭空臆造\n的常识性诱饵。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音多次出现 flexibility、flexible，但讲的是员工要灵活应变，并未说大家弹性工时；\nPeople usually work flexible hours. 借 flexible 同根词诱你联想到工时，无据。\n借用原文：Employees who are flexible demonstrate other skills too.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What are employees with adaptability able to do when changes occur?", "options": ["A) Leave the group as soon as possible.", "B) Anticipate setbacks well in advance.", "C) Decide on new priorities speedily.", "D) Stick to original goals confidently."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Decide on new priorities speedily.\n【定位】They can reprioritize quickly when changes occur and suggest additional modifications when\nsomething is not working.\n当变化发生时，他们能迅速重新排定优先级，并在某些做法行不通时提出额外的调整。\n【信号】when changes occur 条件句应答。They can reprioritize quickly when changes occur\n与题干 when changes occur 逐词对应，reprioritize quickly 就是对该情形的直接应答，\n是答案句。\n【替换】reprioritize quickly ↔ Decide on new priorities speedily.\nreprioritize=decide on new priorities，quickly=speedily\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说有适应力的人遇变化会迅速重排优先级、自信适应，Leave the group as soon as\npossible. 的尽快离队是逃避态度，与原文取向相反，且 leave the group 录音未提。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音后句确念了 regroup quickly when a setback occurs，但说的是挫折发生时迅速重\n整，并非 well in advance 提前预判；Anticipate setbacks well in advance. 盗用 setback\n又把时间偷换成事前。\n借用原文：They can also regroup quickly when a setback occurs\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说 adapting to the new situation confidently，confidently 是修饰适应新情况；\nStick to original goals confidently. 搬走 confidently 却接上坚持原目标，恰与适应力相\n反。\n借用原文：adapting to the new situation confidently and without overreacting", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What question does the speaker address in this talk?", "options": ["A) What differentiates people from animals.", "B) Why philosophers disagree with scientists.", "C) Where humans' great cognitive capacity originates.", "D) When being creative becomes a biological mandate."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） What differentiates people from animals.\n【定位】What makes humans different from other species? Some philosophers argue it's morals or\nethics, while some scientists assert it's our greater cognitive development. But I argue that the\nmain difference is our desire to combat routine.\n是什么让人类区别于其他物种？一些哲学家认为是道德或伦理，而一些科学家断言是我们更\n高级的认知发展。但我认为，主要区别在于我们对抗常规的渴望。\n【信号】开篇设问 + But I argue 亮主线。第一段以设问 What makes humans different from\nother species 开场，随后用 But I argue that the main difference is our desire to\ncombat routine 中的转折信号 But 和立场词 I argue 亮出全篇主线——这正是篇章独\n白\"开头点题\"的答案信号。\n【替换】What makes humans different from other species ↔ What differentiates people from animals\n同义改写：different from species = differentiates from animals\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n第一段确实念到 Some philosophers argue it's morals or ethics, while some scientists\nassert it's our greater cognitive development，选项 Why philosophers disagree with\nscientists 就盗用了 philosophers 和 scientists 这两个原词；但讲者只是借两派观点引出\n自己的主张，全篇根本不讨论\"他们为何分歧\"，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：Some philosophers argue it's morals or ethics, while some scientists assert it's our\ngreater cognitive development\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Where humans' great cognitive capacity originates 借用了原文 our greater\ncognitive development 里的 cognitive；但\"认知发展\"是讲者复述并随即用 But 推翻的科\n学家旧观点，讲座既不问它\"从哪来\"，更没把它当主线，纯属盗词诱选。\n借用原文：our greater cognitive development\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 When being creative becomes a biological mandate 搬来了原文 This makes\nbeing creative a biological mandate 的整串短语；但讲者是把\"创造是生物律令\"当作结论\n陈述，从没问它\"何时\"发生，选项用 When 偷换成一个录音没讨论的时机问题。\n借用原文：This makes being creative a biological mandate", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker believe about innovation?", "options": ["A) It is what tells apart two adjacent generations.", "B) It is what sharpens our appetite for novelty.", "C) It is something only geniuses can achieve.", "D) It is something every human being can do."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It is something every human being can do.\n【定位】Now, many people, both laymen and experts, believe that only geniuses innovate. But I\nbelieve that innovation is not something that only a few people do. The innovative drive lives\nin every human brain.\n如今，许多人——无论外行还是专家——都认为只有天才才会创新。但我认为，创新并不是\n只有少数人才能做的事。创新的驱动力存在于每一个人的大脑中。\n【信号】But I believe + not… only a few 的立场转折。讲者先用 many people…believe that\nonly geniuses innovate 摆出大众看法，再以 But I believe that innovation is not\nsomething that only a few people do 中的 But I believe 强力转折亮出己见——转折后\n+ I believe 的句子正是观点题的答案信号。\n【替换】innovation is not something that only a few people do ↔\nIt is something every human being can do\n否定转肯定：not only a few = every human\nThe innovative drive lives in every human brain ↔ every human being can do\nevery human brain = every human being\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It is what tells apart two adjacent generations 借用了原文 distinguish one\ngeneration from the next 里的 generation；但原文说的是\"创新所引发的变化\"区分了代\n际，而非\"创新本身就是用来区分两代人的\"，选项把因果关系改成了创新的定义，答非所\n问。\n借用原文：distinguish one generation from the next\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 It is what sharpens our appetite for novelty 拼用了第二段的 our appetite for\nnovelty；但原文是\"为满足对新奇的渴望，创新是关键\"，是渴望催生创新，选项却反过来\n说\"创新激起对新奇的渴望\"，因果颠倒、且并非讲者对创新的核心看法。\n借用原文：To satisfy our appetite for novelty, innovation is key\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 It is something only geniuses can achieve 几乎照抄原文 only geniuses\ninnovate；但这恰是讲者用 But I believe 明确推翻的大众看法，是\"与原文相反\"——把讲\n者反对的观点当成讲者的观点，正好选反。\n借用原文：many people…believe that only geniuses innovate", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "How does the human brain work according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) It seeks inspiration for novel inventions.", "B) It constantly absorbs new information.", "C) It uses existing ideas to create new ones.", "D) It repeats precedent on a regular basis."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It uses existing ideas to create new ones.\n【定位】The human brain works from precedent. We take the ideas we've inherited and put them\ntogether into some new shape.\n人脑依凭先例而运作。我们把继承来的想法拿来，重新组合成某种新的形态。\n【信号】直陈机制句 works from + take… put… into new shape。讲者用 The human brain\nworks from precedent 直接点出机制，又以 We take the ideas we've inherited and put\nthem together into some new shape 展开\"拿旧的拼成新的\"——这种正面解释\"怎么运\n作\"的句子就是细节题的答案信号。\n【替换】take the ideas we've inherited and put them together into some new shape ↔\nuses existing ideas to create new ones\n继承的旧想法拼成新形状 = 用已有想法造新的\nit's really the result of developing the ideas of others further ↔\nuses existing ideas to create new ones\n在他人想法上further发展 = 用已有想法造新\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It seeks inspiration for novel inventions 拼用了原文 inspiration 和 one invention\nenables or inspires further inventions 里的词；但讲者明说新想法\"几乎像魔法般灵感闪\n现\"只是大众误解，他真正主张的是\"靠先例\"，故\"四处寻找灵感\"并非讲者的机制描述。\n借用原文：They come in a flash of inspiration to a select few\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 constantly absorbs new information 听上去像\"大脑怎么运作\"的合理答案，且\nabsorb 一词原文第五段确实念到 absorb the past；但原文说的是\"吸收过去/旧的东西\"，\n选项偷换成\"不断吸收新信息\"，方向正好相反，差在 past 被改成 new information。\n借用原文：absorb the past and manipulate it to create possible futures\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 repeats precedent on a regular basis 借走了原文 works from precedent 的\nprecedent；但讲者说的是\"依凭先例去拼出新东西\"，强调的是\"造新\"，选项却说成\"只是定\n期重复先例\"，把创造性的\"用旧造新\"歪曲为机械重复，与原意相反。\n借用原文：The human brain works from precedent", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What view of many dog owners win support from researchers studying dog cognition?", "options": ["A) Dogs know when their owners are not feeling well.", "B) Dogs have the cognition for telling right from wrong.", "C) Dogs have an aptitude for developing skills to interact with humans.", "D) Dogs know when their human companions can no longer stand them."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Dogs know when their owners are not feeling well.\n【定位】Many dog owners would tell you that their dog somehow knows when they're ill or upset, and\naccording to researchers who study dog cognition, those pet owners are right.\n许多狗主人会告诉你，他们的狗不知怎么就能知道他们生病或心烦了；而据研究狗的认知的\n研究者所言，这些主人是对的。\n【信号】研究者的认定语 those pet owners are right。答案落在研究者对狗主人看法的直接认定\nthose pet owners are right 上——\"研究者支持哪个看法\"的答案，就藏在他们说\"对\"所指\n向的那句话里，即狗能知道主人 ill or upset。\n【替换】their dog somehow knows when they're ill or upset ↔\nDogs know when their owners are not feeling well\nill or upset 概括为 not feeling well\n【排除】B) 未提及\ntelling right from wrong（分辨对错）说的是道德认知，录音通篇讲狗\"感知主人健康/情\n绪\"，从未谈狗能分辨是非，属凭常识脑补。\nC) 原词陷阱\nan aptitude for developing skills to interact with humans 借用了录音里真实出现的 an\naptitude for detecting 中的 aptitude，但录音说的是\"察觉变化的天赋\"，不是\"与人互动\n的技能\"，是听到 aptitude 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：domestic dogs have shown an aptitude for detecting\nD) 未提及\nknow when their human companions can no longer stand them（知道主人受不了它\n们了）录音从未提及，且与全文\"狗体察主人健康\"的暖意基调相反，属臆造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Why can dogs detect their owner's abnormal odor according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) They can readily detect different ill smells of viruses.", "B) They can easily tell what bacteria cause odor change.", "C) They are particularly sensitive to strange smells.", "D) They have an extremely powerful sense of smell."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have an extremely powerful sense of smell.\n【定位】If a person is infected with a virus or bacteria, for example, their odor will be abnormal, and\ndogs are able to smell that change even if the human can't, because dogs have a much more\npowerful sense of smell than humans.\n比如，如果一个人感染了病毒或细菌，他的气味就会变得异常，而狗能闻出这种变化，即便\n人闻不到，因为狗的嗅觉比人强大得多。\n【信号】因果信号 because 引出原因。答案紧跟因果词 because 之后——\"狗为什么闻得出\"的原因\n就是 dogs have a much more powerful sense of smell than humans，because 后那句\n即落点。\n【替换】dogs have a much more powerful sense of smell than humans ↔\nThey have an extremely powerful sense of smell\nmore powerful than humans 等于 extremely powerful\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ndetect different ill smells of viruses 借了录音 a virus or bacteria 里的 virus，但录音只\n说狗能闻出气味\"变了\"，从没说狗能\"区分不同病毒的气味\"，是把原词夸大的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：If a person is infected with a virus or bacteria\nB) 原词陷阱\ntell what bacteria cause odor change 用了录音的 bacteria 和 odor change，但录音说\n狗\"闻得出气味变了\"，并非\"判断是哪种细菌导致的\"，问的方向被偷换。\n借用原文：their odor will be abnormal\nC) 答非所问\nparticularly sensitive to strange smells（对怪味敏感）看似合理，但 Why 题答案严格落\n在 because 后的 powerful sense of smell；录音此处归因于\"嗅觉强大\"而非\"对怪味敏\n感\"，C 是和 D 切不开的近义干扰。\n借用原文：dogs are extremely sensitive to changes in the people they're familiar with", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does research suggest for now about dog ownership?", "options": ["A) It can ensure owners suffer fewer chronic diseases.", "B) It can benefit owners both physically and mentally.", "C) It can reduce owners' risk of getting cancer or diabetes.", "D) It can alert owners to the seriousness of their conditions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It can benefit owners both physically and mentally.\n【定位】For now, research suggests dog ownership can have an array of benefits in and of itself.\nKeeping a pet dog has been shown to bolster health and boost mood.\n就目前而言，研究表明养狗本身就能带来一系列益处。养只宠物狗已被证明能增进健康、提\n振心情。\n【信号】时间界标 For now 切出当下结论。末段先说诊病\"在遥远未来\"，再用 For now 转回当下，\n答案落在它后面的 bolster health and boost mood——增进健康（身体）+ 提振心情\n（心理）。\n【替换】bolster health and boost mood ↔ benefit owners both physically and mentally\nhealth+mood 归纳为 physically+mentally\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nensure owners suffer fewer chronic diseases 借了录音的 chronic diseases，但原文说\n狗能给\"患慢病者\"以慰藉 a particular comfort to those with chronic diseases，不是说\n养狗能\"减少患慢病\"，方向被偷换，且 ensure 也过强。\n借用原文：they can be a particular comfort to those with chronic diseases\nC) 原词陷阱\nreduce owners' risk of getting cancer or diabetes 借了录音 diabetes and certain\ntypes of cancer，但那是讲狗\"诊断\"疾病、且明说在 distant future，并非 for now 的结\n论；题问 for now，C 取错了时间段。\n借用原文：detect an assortment of ailments, including diabetes and certain types of cancer\nD) 未提及\nalert owners to the seriousness of their conditions（提醒病情有多严重）录音从未说\n过；末段讲的是养狗\"增进健康、提振心情、给予慰藉\"，没有\"警示病情严重程度\"一说。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does Onward's report propose ministers should do?", "options": ["A) Crack down on courses like science, technology, engineering and math.", "B) Restrict the ability of creative arts courses to recruit new students.", "C) Look at how to reform technical and vocational education.", "D) Ensure creative arts students get better value for money."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Restrict the ability of creative arts courses to recruit new students.\n【定位】Ministers, according to Onward, should \"crack down on courses that offer extremely\nlimited value for money to students ten years after graduation\", restricting the ability of\nsuch courses to recruit new students, if the average graduate earns below the student loans\npayment threshold.\n据 Onward 称，大臣们应当\"严打那些在学生毕业十年后仍只能提供极有限性价比的课程\"，\n即在这类课程毕业生平均收入低于助学贷款偿还门槛时，限制它们招收新生的能力。\n【信号】建议信号 should，紧跟报告主张。Ministers, according to Onward, should 里的\naccording to Onward + should 双重标记，明确这是\"报告建议大臣做什么\"，正是题干所\n问，后半 restricting the ability of such courses to recruit new students 即具体动作。\n【替换】restricting the ability of such courses to recruit new students ↔\nRestrict the ability of creative arts courses to recruit new students.\n几乎原句保留，such courses 具体化为 creative arts courses\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项盗用了原文 crack down on courses 和 science, technology, engineering, and\nmath 两处原词，但原文 crack down 针对的是低回报课程，而理工科恰恰是 should be\nfavored（应受青睐）。把\"打压\"安到理工科头上，正负方向全反。\n借用原文：Courses like science, technology, engineering, and math, and economics, where the\naverage graduate earns a lot, should be favored.\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项盗用了第二段 looks at how to reform technical education 的原词，但那是 a\ngovernment review（政府评估）的工作内容，并非 Onward 报告\"提议大臣做\"的事。题\n干问的是报告 propose 什么，主体被偷换。\n借用原文：The report provides insight into a government review which looks at how to reform\ntechnical education\nD) 偷换概念\n选项里 24D 式的 value for money 措辞来自 ensure students get good value for\nmoney，但那同样是政府评估关注的目标，且报告主张的是\"限制招生\"这一具体动作，而\n非\"确保创意艺术学生物有所值\"。把整体评估目标偷换成报告的提议。\n借用原文：how to ensure students get good value for money", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker think of Onward's arguments?", "options": ["A) Seemingly reasonable.", "B) Clearly well-grounded.", "C) Extremely irrational.", "D) Apparently simplistic."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） Seemingly reasonable.\n【定位】At first glance, it might even seem like Onward have a point.\n乍一看，Onward 的观点甚至似乎还有几分道理。\n【信号】让步信号 At first glance + might seem，暗示\"表面有理\"。At first glance, it might\neven seem like Onward have a point. 中 At first glance（乍看）与 might even seem\n（似乎）连用，是典型的\"表面看…\"让步语气，说明作者承认其\"看似有理\"，但保留实质判\n断——后文随即用数据与反驳推翻它。\n【替换】At first glance, it might even seem like Onward have a point. ↔ Seemingly reasonable.\nseem like...have a point ↔ Seemingly reasonable，详述概括化\n【排除】B)\nClearly well-grounded. 把作者的 seem like（似乎）拔高成 Clearly（明显）、well-\ngrounded（根据充分）。原文有 At first glance\\might even seem 的限定，恰恰说明作\n者认为它经不起推敲，并非\"明显有理\"。\n借用原文：it might even seem like Onward have a point\nC) 过度推断\nExtremely irrational. 把作者温和的\"看似有理但有问题\"夸张成\"极不理性\"。原文是 might\neven seem like...have a point，承认其表面合理，远没到 extremely irrational 的全盘否\n定，态度强度严重失真。\nD) 未提及\nApparently simplistic. 的 Apparently 蹭了原文\"表面看\"的味道，但 simplistic（过于简单\n化）这一评价原文从未给出——作者的批评是\"问题不在具体学位而在整体经济\"，是归因偏\n差，而非\"简单化\"。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about British college graduates five years after graduation?", "options": ["A) A high proportion of them haven't tried to save money.", "B) Most of them never hope to buy a house or to retire.", "C) Forty percent of them earn less than ￡25,000 a year.", "D) The majority of them have fairly well-paying jobs."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Forty percent of them earn less than ￡25,000 a year.\n【定位】Well, 40 percent of all graduates—regardless of their degree—are on less than 25,000 pounds\na year, 5 years after graduation.\n要知道，所有毕业生中——无论学的什么专业——有 40% 的人在毕业 5 年后年收入低于\n25000 英镑。\n【信号】精确限定三连：40% + all graduates + 5 years after。40 percent of all graduates—\nregardless of their degree—are on less than 25,000 pounds a year, 5 years after\ngraduation. 同时给出百分比、人群（all graduates）、时间（5 years），三项限定与题干\nfive years after graduation 完全对齐，是唯一匹配句。\n【替换】40 percent of all graduates ↔ Forty percent of them\n数字逐字对应，all graduates ↔ them\nless than 25,000 pounds a year ↔ less than ￡25,000 a year\n金额与范围词原样保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了 never save up 的原词，但原文是\"被迫过上永远存不下钱的生活\"，说的是\"现\n状被迫如此\"，并非 haven't tried to save money（没尝试去存钱）。把\"存不下\"偷换成\"没\n去尝试存\"，且该描述属 P2 的创意艺术生、非本题 5 年后的全体毕业生。\n借用原文：never save up, never buy a house, never hope to retire\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用 never buy a house never hope to retire 的原词，但原文是\"被迫过上永远买不\n起房、不敢指望退休的生活\"，是被环境所迫的处境，不是 never hope to buy a house or\nto retire（主观上不想买房退休）。且这同样是 P2 描述创意艺术生的，与题干 5 年后全体\n毕业生不符。\n借用原文：never buy a house, never hope to retire\nD) 与原文相反\nThe majority of them have fairly well-paying jobs. 蹭了 P3 fairly good work\nprospects（顶尖名校创意艺术生前景不错）的原词，但那只针对牛津等顶尖名校的少数\n人；就全体毕业生而言原文恰恰说 40% 低于 25000，多数收入并不高。把局部的\"前景\n好\"放大成整体\"多数高薪\"，方向相反。\n借用原文：Creative arts graduates from top universities—like Oxford...—have fairly good\nwork prospects.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say actually accounts for the problem identified by Onward?", "options": ["A) The context of a bank balance.", "B) Britain's economy as a whole.", "C) The specific degree a student earns.", "D) Britain's defective educational system."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Britain's economy as a whole.\n【定位】This suggests that the problem isn't really to do with specific students studying specific\ndegrees, but really with the economy as a whole.\n这表明，问题其实并不在于具体学生读了什么具体专业，而真正在于整体经济。\n【信号】对照信号 isn't...but...，真因在 but 之后。the problem isn't really to do with specific\nstudents studying specific degrees, but really with the economy as a whole. 用\nisn't...but... 先否掉\"具体专业\"这个归因，再把真正原因点在 but 之后的 the economy as a\nwhole，正对题干 actually accounts for。\n【替换】the economy as a whole ↔ Britain's economy as a whole.\neconomy as a whole 几乎原词，补限定 Britain's\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe context of a bank balance. 盗用了 P4 the value of education not in the context\nof a bank balance 的原词，但原文恰恰说\"不要在银行账户的语境里、而要在人生的语境里\n看教育的价值\"，bank balance 是作者要否定的衡量方式，而非问题根源。\n借用原文：not in the context of a bank balance, but of a life\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe specific degree a student earns. 盗用 specific students studying specific degrees\n的原词，且顺着前文\"创意艺术学位不值钱\"最像答案；但原文 isn't really to do with\nspecific...degrees 已把它明确否定，真因在 but 之后，故它正是要排的\"but 前被否定\n项\"。\n借用原文：the problem isn't really to do with specific students studying specific degrees\nD) 未提及\nBritain's defective educational system. 听着像总结，但原文从未说\"英国教育制度有缺\n陷\"是问题根源；作者归因是 the economy as a whole（整体经济），并在 P4 主张到\"高\n等教育之外的更广世界\"去看，并未指控教育系统 defective。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) flat\n形容词\n【词性槽】a ___ stretch of land，冠词后名词前作定语\n【依据】flat 表\"平坦的、单调的\"，呼应\"路过者只觉无趣\"\n前文铺垫 it looks lifeless, barren, and bone-dry，路过者的视角下沙漠是\"一片平坦无\n奇\"的土地，flat 既符合视觉特征又含\"乏味\"色彩，与 \"some mountains in the distance\"\n远景一片低平相对照。\n【竞争词】O) unique unique 是褒义\"独特的\"，与路过者负面初印象冲突\n本句站在\"匆匆路过、误解沙漠\"的视角，褒义词不合逻辑；unique 真正贴合的是 35 题描写沙漠\n真实风貌处。\nM) sturdy sturdy 常修饰人/物结实，不修饰 stretch of land\nsturdy 用来形容鞋、家具、人结实耐用，与 \"一片土地\" 不搭。\nA) apt apt 后常跟 to/at，单独作定语少见\napt 作定语虽语法可行（恰当的），但语义上\"恰当的一片土地\"没说清啥；且 A 在后文\"more\n___ to be a setting\" 句里和 to 搭配更合身，应留给那处。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— unique 是褒义，与路过者负面初印象相反。unique 词性\n也对、放进 \"a ___ stretch of land\" 句也通，但本句视角是\"误解沙漠的路过者\"，褒义形容\n词违和；忽略说话人态度色彩就会被钓走。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a ___ stretch of lan"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) apt\n形容词\n【词性槽】more ___ to be，比较级框架后形容词\n【依据】be apt to do = 易于/倾向于做某事\nbe apt to do 是固定结构，意为\"很可能/适合于……\"，与 more 连用表\"更像/更适合作火星\n电影背景\"，配合 more __(27)__ to be a setting for a movie that takes place on Mars.\n上下文极顺。\n【竞争词】O) unique unique 不接 to do\nunique 后接 to 时表\"为……所独有\"（unique to X），不接 to do 不定式；且 unique 是绝对级\n形容词，语义上也不与 more 自然搭配。\nM) sturdy sturdy 不接 to do，且不修饰场景\nsturdy 没有 \"be sturdy to do\" 的固定结构，语义也偏\"结实耐用\"，与\"像火星电影场景\"扯\n不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·more ___ to be，比较级框架"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) overflowing\n形容词\n【词性槽】be ... with 结构，空处作表语形容词\n【依据】be overflowing with 表\"充满、洋溢着\"，与转折语义吻合\n首句说沙漠看似 lifeless, barren, and bone-dry，然后 however 转折，需要一个表\"满满\n都是\"的强对比形容词，overflowing 与 with 构成固定搭配 \"be overflowing with\"，正好\n与 life、mystery、magic 这一串积极名词配伍。\n【竞争词】N) synonymous 搭配虽对，但语义指向\"等同\"而非\"充满\"\nbe synonymous with 意为\"与……同义/紧密关联\"，放进 is __(28)__ with life, mystery, and\nmagic. 会变成\"沙漠等同于生命、神秘与魔法\"，语义太绝对，且后文段 31 才真正用到\nsynonymous，更切合那里。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— synonymous with 也成立，但语义太强、抢了 31 题的\n位置。synonymous 与 with 搭配看似也对，会把考生骗进\"沙漠等同于生命与神秘\"的\n过度判断；记住做选词填空要全篇预判搭配归属，把唯一性最强的搭配留给真正的位置\n（31 题）。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be ... with 结构，空处作表语"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) extremes\n名词\n【词性槽】a land of ___，of 后填名词\n【依据】a land of extremes = 极端反差之地\n后文 Temperature fluctuations vary from freezing to __(30)__ hot, not only between\nseasons, but even within the same day. 正好解释了\"极端\"——温差从冰点跨到酷热，\nextremes 既匹配语义又是英语里常见的固定表达 \"a land of extremes\"。\n【竞争词】E) fractions fractions 指\"分数/小部分\"，与沙漠特征无关\n\"a land of fractions\" 没有这种说法，也无法解释后文的温度反差，语义完全跑偏。\nG) parasites parasites 寄生虫，与沙漠语境不符\n把沙漠说成\"寄生虫之地\"语义离奇，且后文谈的是温度而非生物，逻辑指向不对。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a land of ___，of 后填名"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) burning\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰形容词 hot\n【依据】burning hot 表\"灼热\"，与 freezing 形成强对比\n前文是 freezing（冰冻）极端冷，空处需要与之对称的极端热表达，burning 常以 burning\nhot 形式强调\"灼热\"，呼应 29 题的 a land of extremes。\n【竞争词】H) probably probably 表可能性，与温度程度无关\n\"freezing to probably hot\" 逻辑荒谬：极端温度对比里不会说\"大概热\"，且 probably 也无法\n与 freezing 形成对称的程度感。\nI) sparingly sparingly 指\"节俭地\"，语义完全错位\nsparingly 用来修饰使用、消费等动作，放在 hot 前\"节俭地热\"毫无意义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词修饰形容词 hot"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) synonymous\n形容词\n【词性槽】have become ___ with，空作表语形容词\n【依据】be/become synonymous with = 成为……的代名词\n宽边帽 the wide-brimmed(宽边的) hats 与优胜美地等国家公园在大众认知里已经画等\n号，synonymous 完美贴合 \"become synonymous with places like Yosemite\" 这一固定\n表达。\n【竞争词】A) apt apt 不与 with 搭配\napt 常说 be apt to do/be apt at，搭配是 to 或 at，不与 with 连用，语法上首先就被排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·have become ___ with"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) stimulate\n动词\n【词性槽】to ___ your interest，不定式后接动词原形\n【依据】stimulate one's interest = 激发某人的兴趣\nstimulate 是 interest 的高频搭配动词，意为\"激发兴趣\"，与\"无尽的机会去激发你的兴\n趣\"完全吻合，且前文 rich with history, culture, biodiversity, and endless opportunities\n也在铺垫\"种种激发兴趣的资源\"。\n【竞争词】J) sprinkle sprinkle 表\"撒、洒\"，与 interest 不搭\nsprinkle one's interest 不是英语自然表达，sprinkle 宾语多为水、糖粉、点缀物等具体物质。\nK) steer steer 是\"驾驶/引导\"，宾语常为人、船、方向\nsteer your interest 虽勉强可懂，但搭配生硬；英语里\"激发兴趣\"的固定动词是\nstimulate/arouse/spark，不会用 steer。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·to ___ your interest"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) probably\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰整句/动词性结构 a few spare tires to maneuver...\n【依据】probably 表\"很可能\"，符合作者经验之谈的口吻\n作者在分享在保护区拍照的经验：耐心是必须，备胎\"很可能\"也需要，probably 正好传达\n这种\"建议但留余地\"的语气。\n【竞争词】I) sparingly sparingly 与名词 a few spare tires 语义冲突\nsparingly 意为\"节制地、少量地\"，但句中已经用 a few 限定数量，再加 sparingly 既冗余又改\n变重心；语义上也与\"建议多备\"的语境相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词修饰整句/动词性结构 a few s"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) sturdy\n形容词\n【词性槽】a good, ___ pair of shoes 并列修饰\n【依据】sturdy 形容鞋\"结实耐用\"，与 good 同向\n沙漠拍照路况差 the network of unpaved \"roads\"，鞋需要\"结实\"才扛得住，sturdy 与\ngood 形成自然的双形容词修饰：\"一双好的、结实的鞋\"。\n【竞争词】O) unique unique 修饰鞋偏向\"独特款式\"，与实用语境不符\n沙漠摄影准备讲的是耐用、防护，不是款式独特；unique 真正切题之处在 35 题描写沙漠地貌。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a good, ___ pair of"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apt", "B) burning", "C) extremes", "D) flat", "E) fractions", "F) overflowing", "G) parasites", "H) probably", "I) sparingly", "J) sprinkle", "K) steer", "L) stimulate", "M) sturdy", "N) synonymous", "O) unique"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) unique\n形容词\n【词性槽】giving the area a ___ feel，名词前定语\n【依据】unique feel = 独一无二的感觉\n前文 much of the area was shaped by ancient volcanoes and geological forces 强调\n这片土地由古火山与地质力量塑造，留给观者\"独特\"的观感，unique 与彩色摄影背景描述\n完美衔接。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·giving the area a __"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is said that agricultural productivity in many African countries has remained low for a century.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一百年未提升的农业生产率\n【定位】Farm productivity hasn't improved in many parts of Africa for 100 years.\n非洲许多地方的农业生产率已经一百年没有改善了。\n【改写】agricultural productivity in many African countries has remained low for a century ↔\nFarm productivity hasn't improved in many parts of Africa for 100 years\n数字单位换写 + 否定改善 = 维持低位\n【辨邻】 D D 段 output per worker has improved by more than half over the past 30 years\nin Africa 给的时间是 30 年、方向是「提升过半」，与题干 remained low for a\ncentury 的「停滞一百年」结论完全相反，属于只覆盖局部 + 方向相反，不是源\n头段。\noutput per worker has improved by more than half over the past 30 years in Africa, that is still far\nbehind the 2.5 times improvement in Asia", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Building connections between farms and major purchasers of their produce can promote African farmers' use of advanced farming techniques.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】把农场连到大买家以推广技术\n【定位】One way of spreading knowledge is to link farms to big buyers of their harvests.\n传播知识的一种方式，是把农场与他们农产品的大买家连起来。\n【改写】Building connections between farms and major purchasers of their produce ↔\nlink farms to big buyers of their harvests\n建立连接 = link；大买家 = big buyers；农产品 = harvests\ncan promote African farmers' use of advanced farming techniques ↔\nOne way of spreading knowledge\n传播知识即让农民用上先进做法\n【辨邻】 F F 段重心在 getting small farmers to use fertiliser and, more important, better\nseeds，讲的是「输入端」做法，没有「把农场连到大买家」这件事，与题干 Building\nconnections between farms and major purchasers 的渠道连接路径不同。\nMuch of the focus has been on getting small farmers to use fertiliser and, more important,\nbetter seeds\nG G 段 Many of their neighbours do not follow the advice 走的是「邻里看效果再\n跟」的路径，与 Building connections between farms and major purchasers 的\n「连到买家」机制不同。\nSmall coffee farmers in Kenya are able to increase their incomes by 40% by following a few simple\nguidelines on caring for their bushes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Parts of Africa are getting warmer much faster than the average, with southern Africa witnessing roughly twice the global warming rate over the last half century.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】南部非洲约两倍全球升温速度\n【定位】Parts of the continent are already warming much more quickly than the average: temperatures\nin southern Africa have increased by about twice the global rate over the past 50 years.\n非洲部分地区已经在以远高于平均的速度变暖：过去 50 年里，南部非洲的气温升幅约为全\n球升温速率的两倍。\n【改写】southern Africa witnessing roughly twice the global warming rate ↔\ntemperatures in southern Africa have increased by about twice the global rate\nroughly = about，witness 等价于 have increased by 的概括说法\nover the last half century ↔ over the past 50 years\nhalf century = 50 years\nParts of Africa are getting warmer much faster than the average ↔\nParts of the continent are already warming much more quickly than the average\ncontinent = Africa 上下位换名；getting warmer = warming", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Improved farming practices have enabled Kenyan farmers to increase farm produce remarkably.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】改良耕作法显著提高肯尼亚农产量\n【定位】Small coffee farmers in Kenya are able to increase their incomes by 40% by following a few\nsimple guidelines on caring for their bushes, such as trimming all but three of their stems.\n肯尼亚的小咖啡农只要遵循几条简单的护树建议——比如把茎全部修剪到只剩三根——就能\n把收入提高 40%。\n【改写】Improved farming practices ↔ a few simple guidelines on caring for their bushes\n上位词 farming practices 覆盖下位的 simple guidelines\nKenyan farmers ↔ Small coffee farmers in Kenya\nKenyan farmers 与 farmers in Kenya 等价\nincrease farm produce remarkably ↔ increase their incomes by 40%\n40% 即 remarkably；produce 与 incomes 通过因果换皮\n【辨邻】 F F 段 Improved varieties of sorghum 与 from about 8 tonnes per hectare to 35\ntonnes 讲的是高粱与马铃薯增产，不是肯尼亚咖啡农剪枝的故事，主体不同。\nImproved varieties of sorghum, for instance, can produce a crop that is 40% larger than the\nusual variety\nH H 段 link farms to big buyers of their harvests 走的是渠道型推广，不是题干\nImproved farming practices 的耕作做法改良。\nWhen Diageo, a British drinks giant, built a brewery in western Kenya", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Africa is especially susceptible to the effects of global warming partly because it has difficulty feeding its increasing population even without climate change.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】气候变化叠加人口增长的双重脆弱\n【定位】Africa is particularly vulnerable, in part because it is already struggling to feed itself and it\nwill have to vastly increase yields and productivity if it is to put food on the plates of a fast-\ngrowing population, even without climate change.\n非洲格外脆弱，部分原因是它本就难以养活自己，而且即便没有气候变化，它也得大幅提高\n产量和生产率，才能喂饱一个快速增长的人口。\n【改写】especially susceptible to the effects of global warming ↔ particularly vulnerable\nespecially = particularly；susceptible = vulnerable\nhas difficulty feeding its increasing population ↔ struggling to feed itself\nhas difficulty doing = struggling to do；fast-growing 同义 increasing\neven without climate change ↔ even without climate change\n短语原样复现\n【辨邻】 B B 段 About 40% of the land now used to grow maize would no longer be\nsuitable for it 给的是气候导致的种植带变化，而题干 has difficulty feeding its\nincreasing population even without climate change 要的是「即使没气候变化也喂\n不饱」的人口端理由，方向不同。\nEven if the world were to cut emissions enough to keep global warming below 1.5℃, heat-waves\nwould intensify in Africa", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The use of fertiliser and improved seeds can help Africa's small farmers impressively increase crop yields.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】化肥与改良种子帮小农大幅增产\n【定位】Much of the focus has been on getting small farmers to use fertiliser and, more important,\nbetter seeds.\n（重新重视农业的）重点很大程度上落在让小农使用化肥，以及更重要的——使用更好的种\n子上。\n【改写】The use of fertiliser and improved seeds ↔\ngetting small farmers to use fertiliser and, more important, better seeds\nfertiliser 原样 + improved seeds = better seeds\ncan help Africa's small farmers impressively increase crop yields ↔\nThe results can be impressive\nimpressively 与 impressive 同根同义\n【辨邻】 G G 段 trimming all but three of their stems 走的是修剪指南，不是题干 The use of\nfertiliser and improved seeds 要的两类输入。\nby following a few simple guidelines on caring for their bushes, such as trimming all but three of\ntheir stems", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "It has proved even more important to shift from traditional farming to setting up businesses that can bring technology and investment to small farmers in Africa.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】从传统耕作转向科技+投资的商业模式\n【定位】An even more important change is the move from traditional farming to building businesses\nthat can profitably bring technology and investment to small farmers.\n一个更重要的变化是：从传统耕作转向建立那些能把技术与投资带给小农、且能赚钱的\n企业。\n【改写】shift from traditional farming to setting up businesses ↔\nthe move from traditional farming to building businesses\nshift = move；setting up = building\nbring technology and investment to small farmers ↔\nprofitably bring technology and investment to small farmers\n除 profitably 外短语逐词一致\nIt has proved even more important ↔ An even more important change\n「更重要」整体平移\n【辨邻】 J J 段 Illuminum's success shows how technology can help even small farms\nbecome more productive 是对 I 段案例的提炼，并不重新陈述「从传统耕作转向建\n立企业」这件事；题干 shift from traditional farming to setting up businesses 的\n源头是 I 段那句首提的转型断言。\nIlluminum's success shows how technology can help even small farms become more productive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Everyone in the world will have to bear the consequences of climate change, especially Africans.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】全人类共担、非洲尤其要承受气候后果\n【定位】Climate change is an issue that will affect everyone on the planet. For Africans its\nconsequences will be particularly bitter.\n气候变化是一个会影响地球上每个人的议题，但对非洲人来说，其后果将尤其苦涩。\n【改写】Everyone in the world will have to bear the consequences of climate change ↔\nClimate change is an issue that will affect everyone on the planet\neveryone in the world = everyone on the planet；bear consequences = be affected\nespecially Africans ↔ For Africans its consequences will be particularly bitter\nespecially = particularly（程度副词同义）\n【辨邻】 B B 段 global warming represents one of the main threats to Africa's prosperity 是\n具体威胁论证，与题干 Everyone in the world will have to bear the consequences\n的「全体共担」总论调性不对位。\nglobal warming represents one of the main threats to Africa's prosperity", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Improvement in farm output per worker in Africa falls far short of that in Asia.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】非洲人均产出提升远落后于亚洲\n【定位】And although output per worker has improved by more than half over the past 30 years in\nAfrica, that is still far behind the 2.5 times improvement in Asia.\n尽管过去 30 年里非洲的人均产出提升了一半以上，这仍远落后于亚洲的 2.5 倍提升。\n【改写】Improvement in farm output per worker in Africa ↔\noutput per worker has improved by more than half over the past 30 years in Africa\n动词句 → 名词化短语\nfalls far short of that in Asia ↔ far behind the 2.5 times improvement in Asia\nfall far short of = far behind\n【辨邻】 E E 段 Farm productivity hasn't improved in many parts of Africa for 100 years 没\n有提到亚洲，与题干 falls far short of that in Asia 中作为对比标杆的亚洲缺失，不是\n源头段。\nFarm productivity hasn't improved in many parts of Africa for 100 years", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "In the long term, the potential for small farms in Africa to increase productivity is quite limited, especially owing to the warming climate and a growing population.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】长期看小农增产潜力有限\n【定位】But over the longer run small-scale farming can go only so far, especially in the face of\nclimate change and population pressure.\n但从更长远看，小规模农业能走的也就这么远，尤其是面对气候变化和人口压力的时候。\n【改写】In the long term, the potential for small farms in Africa to increase productivity is quite limited ↔\nover the longer run small-scale farming can go only so far\nlong term = longer run；quite limited = can go only so far\nespecially owing to the warming climate and a growing population ↔\nespecially in the face of climate change and population pressure\nowing to = in the face of；warming climate ≈ climate change；growing population ≈\npopulation pressure\n【辨邻】 I I 段 Illuminum Greenhouses, has sold more than 1,400 greenhouses that\nprovide livelihoods to about 6,000 people 给的是「小农通过科技获得收益」的正\n面案例，与题干 the potential for small farms in Africa to increase productivity is\nquite limited 的「潜力有限」断言方向相反，是 42 题的归属段而非本题。\nAn even more important change is the move from traditional farming to building businesses that\ncan profitably bring technology and investment to small farmers\nK K 段 Projects that give them an extra $2 a month from growing beans or maize\naren't going to get them there 给的是收入门槛论证，没有「长期看 + 气候变化 +\n人口」这套与题干 especially owing to the warming climate and a growing\npopulation 完全对位的双因表述。\nGetting those big jumps will need better jobs in factories and cities", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why does the formula of tax cuts remain popular though ineffective?", "options": ["A) Its critics' voice has not been heard throughout the country.", "B) There seem to be no other options available to replace it.", "C) The cult of tax cuts has been relentlessly propagated by all policymakers.", "D) There appears to be a misunderstanding of the formula among the public."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the formula of tax cuts remain popular though ineffective? 用 Why 直接问\n原因，是要回到原文找一句明确解释。\n【拆句】主句 Why does the formula of tax cuts remain popular 加状语从句 though ineffective\n——though 后省略了 it is，完整是 though it is ineffective；六级常用 though 引出让步\n状语，识别它是看懂题干逻辑的关键。\n【定位】And while the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics, it lacks for obvious rivals.\n尽管对减税的崇拜招致了许多批评，但它（在政策市场上）没有明显的替代品。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说 the cult of tax cuts has attracted many critics——招致很多批评，没有说\n这些批评\"未被全国听见\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nit lacks for obvious rivals ↔ There seem to be no other options available to replace it.\nB 项是 it lacks for obvious rivals 的同义重写，与预判完全吻合。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文 One of the great successes of the Republican Party 明确只是共和党在传播，\n选项扩大为 all policymakers（所有政策制定者）。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只说该公式 doesn't work, but that has not affected its popularity，并未说大\n众\"误解\"了这个公式。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author think is a more effective measure for driving economic growth in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) Aiding people left behind by economic growth.", "B) Prioritizing the growth of the nation's GDP.", "C) Increasing the compensation for labor.", "D) Introducing even more extensive tax cuts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think is a more effective measure for driving economic\ngrowth in the U.S.? 直接问作者认为有效的措施，原文必有一句明确给出。\n【拆句】主句 What does the author think，think 后跟省略 that 的宾语从句 is a more effective\nmeasure for driving economic growth in the U.S.；六级常考插入语式的 author think，\n识别它后面才是真正提问的内容。\n【定位】Raising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic policymakers.\n提高美国工人的工资，应当是经济政策制定者的首要任务。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nDemocratic politicians have tended to campaign on helping people left behind\nby economic growth. 这是作者描述民主党的做法，紧接着 P4 就把它否定为\"政治\n问题\"。\nB) 与原文相反\nWe'd all be better off paying less attention to quarterly updates on the growth of\nthe nation's gross domestic product (GDP)——作者明确说应少关注 GDP 增长。\nC) ✓ 正确\nRaising the wages of American workers ↔ Increasing the compensation for labor.\nC 项是 Raising the wages of American workers 的逐字同义改写，与预判完全吻合。\nD) 与原文相反\n全文要驳斥的就是减税公式（tax cuts），作者不可能反过来主张更大规模减税。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the logic underlying the author's viewpoint?", "options": ["A) The growth of workers' paychecks ultimately boosts the nation's economy.", "B) Paying a premium to maintain a reliable workforce attracts more customers.", "C) Consumption stimulates the desire for higher wages.", "D) Familiar arguments for higher wages are outdated."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the logic underlying the author's viewpoint? 用 What 直接问支撑作者观点的逻\n辑机制，原文应有明确陈述。\n【拆句】What is the logic underlying the author's viewpoint?——underlying 是现在分词作后\n置定语，修饰 logic，等于 that underlies；六级阅读高频，看到 -ing 后置定语要意识到它\n修饰前面那个名词。\n【定位】Consumption drives the American economy, and workers who are paid more can spend more.\n消费驱动着美国经济，而薪酬越高的工人能消费得越多。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nworkers who are paid more can spend more ↔\nThe growth of workers' paychecks ultimately boosts the nation's economy.\nConsumption drives the American economy ↔ ultimately boosts the nation's economy\nA 项用 ultimately 把 P8 的两句话压成一句因果，与预判的\"加薪→消费→经济增长\"完\n全一致。\nB) 偷换概念\nHe did it to increase production—he was paying a premium to maintain a\nreliable workforce. 是福特出于产能目的支付溢价；The unexpected benefit was that\nFord's factory workers became Ford customers, too. 中\"工人变顾客\"是意外结果，\n并非\"支付溢价\"直接导向\"吸引更多顾客\"。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 workers who are paid more can spend more 是高工资 → 高消费，选项反成\"消\n费刺激加薪欲望\"，因果链方向倒置。\nD) 过度推断\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness.——\n作者只是说\"暂且搁置\"公平这一旧论据，并未说它过时。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the basis for higher wages according to the conventional wisdom?", "options": ["A) Fairness in distribution.", "B) Increase in productivity.", "C) The priority of economic policymakers.", "D) The unerring judgment of market forces."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the basis for higher wages according to the conventional wisdom? 用 What\n直问按传统观点加薪的依据，回原文找明确陈述句。\n【拆句】主句 What is the basis for higher wages + 介词短语 according to the conventional\nwisdom——according to 引出陈述主体，意味着答案要看 conventional wisdom 那方怎\n么说，而非作者本人怎么说；六级常借这个介词短语区分立场，须警觉。\n【定位】The conventional wisdom held that productivity growth was the only route to higher wages.\n传统观点认为，生产率的提高是通往更高工资的唯一途径。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nSet aside, for the moment, the familiar argument for higher wages: fairness.\n中\"公平\"是作者列出的另一种加薪理由（且被搁置），与 conventional wisdom 无关。\nB) ✓ 正确\nproductivity growth ↔ Increase in productivity.\nB 项就是 productivity growth 的同义改写，与预判直接对应。\nC) 张冠李戴\nRaising the wages of American workers ought to be the priority of economic\npolicymakers. 是作者本人的主张，不是传统观点对加薪基础的判断。\nD) 张冠李戴\nMainstream economists insist that ... compensation levels reflect the unerring\njudgment of market forces. 是主流经济学家的判断，且原文中\"市场力量的无误判\n断\"是指工资水平的决定方式，不是加薪的依据。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn about things in the real world in America for the past 50 years or so?", "options": ["A) People have failed to see a corresponding increase in wages and in productivity.", "B) People have been disheartened by the widening gap between the haves and have-nots.", "C) People have witnessed a tug of war between Republicans and Democrats over tax cuts.", "D) People have seen the link disappearing between productivity and workers' well-being."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about things in the real world in America for the past 50 years or\nso? 问我们从原文得知的事实，原文必有对应陈述。\n【拆句】What do we learn about things in the real world in America for the past 50 years or\nso?——主干是 What do we learn about things，三层介词短语 in the real world/in\nAmerica/for the past 50 years or so 层层限定 things；六级长题干常用这种层层限定，\n要逐层抓住主体（things=各种情况）。\n【定位】In the real world, things are more complicated. Wages are influenced by a tug of war between\nemployers and workers, and employers have been winning. One clear piece of evidence is the\nwidening gap between productivity growth and wage growth since roughly 1970.\nProductivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind.\n在现实世界里，情况更复杂。工资受到雇主与工人之间拉锯战的影响，而雇主一直在胜出。\n一个明显的证据是，自 1970 年前后以来，生产率增长与工资增长之间的差距越来越大：生\n产率已经翻了一倍多，而工资远远落在后面。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nProductivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind. ↔\nPeople have failed to see a corresponding increase in wages and in productivity.\nthe widening gap between productivity growth and wage growth ↔\na corresponding increase in wages and in productivity\nA 项用 failed to see a corresponding increase 表达\"生产率翻倍而工资落后\"的整体含\n义，与预判完全吻合。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 a tug of war between employers and workers 是劳资博弈，不是贫富分化\n（haves and have-nots）；且原文未提任何人 disheartened（沮丧）。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 a tug of war between employers and workers 是劳资双方的拉锯战，而非两党\n的争斗；且原文未说民主党与共和党在减税上拉锯。\nD) 过度推断\nProductivity has more than doubled; wages have lagged far behind. 说的是\"差距\n扩大\"，并未说\"联系消失（link disappearing）\"——差距大 ≠ 联系断；且 well-being\n（福祉）也比 wages 范围更大。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What would we expect an editorial board of an academic journal to exhibit in view of its important responsibilities?", "options": ["A) Insight.", "B) Expertise.", "C) Integrity.", "D) Diversity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What would we expect an editorial board of an academic journal to exhibit in view\nof its important responsibilities? 问的是「鉴于职责，应当呈现什么」，是原文一句话可\n以直接回答的事实，不含 infer/imply。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What would we expect an editorial board…to exhibit，what 作 exhibit 的\n宾语前置；in view of its important responsibilities 是介词短语作原因状语，可剥离。剥\n离后等价于 We expect an editorial board to exhibit what?，结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】You'd hope, then, that journals would do everything they can to establish a diverse editorial\nboard, reflecting a variety of voices, experiences, and identities.\n那么，你会希望期刊竭尽所能去建立一个多元化的编委会，从而反映各种各样的声音、经验\n与身份。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n全文没有任何句子提到「洞察力」相关概念，Insight 是从职责一词主观联想出来的\n属性。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是编辑的「身份多样性」而非「专业能力」；编辑工作虽要求专业，但全文没\n有任何一句强调专业能力，Expertise 是属性偷换。\nC) 无中生有\n全文未出现「诚信／正直」相关讨论，Integrity 同样是从「重要职责」一词联想出的主\n观属性。\nD) ✓ 正确\ndiverse editorial board ↔ Diversity\n预判语义「多元」与 Diversity 完全同义，其它三项均无对应原文支撑，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn from the findings of a new study in Nature Neuroscience?", "options": ["A) The majority of top psychology and neuroscience journals reflect a variety of voices, experiences and identities.", "B) The editorial boards of most psychology and neuroscience journals do influence the direction their field takes.", "C) The editorial boards of the most important journals in psychology and neuroscience are male-dominated.", "D) The majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals have relevant backgrounds."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the findings of a new study in Nature Neuroscience? 问的是\n研究本身陈述了什么，原文有直陈句，不需要跨段推断。\n【拆句】题干主干 What do we learn from the findings…?，what 作 learn 的宾语前置；from\nthe findings of a new study in Nature Neuroscience 是 of 套 of 的多层介词结构，但只\n是限定 findings 来源，对理解无障碍。\n【定位】The team finds that the majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are\nmale and based in the United States: a situation that may be amplifying existing gender\ninequalities in the field and influencing the kind of research that gets published.\n研究团队发现，顶级心理学与神经科学期刊的大多数编辑是男性，且在美国工作——这种状\n况可能正在放大该领域既有的性别不平等，并影响着哪些研究得以发表。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nreflect a variety of voices, experiences and identities 是 P1 中作者「期望」的状\n态，而 P2 恰恰指出现实并非如此——选项把「期待」当成「现实」，方向完全反了。\nB) 答非所问\ndo influence the direction their field takes 借用 influencing the direction that a\nfield takes，但那是 P1 描述「编辑职责」的总论，不是 P2 的「研究发现」；题干问\nfindings of a new study，本选项答的是 P1 的背景，与 52 题问的范围错位。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe majority of editors in top psychology and neuroscience journals are male ↔\nThe editorial boards of the most important journals in psychology and neuroscience are\nmale-dominated.\ntop psychology and neuroscience journals ↔\nthe most important journals in psychology and neuroscience\n「顶级=最重要」「以男为主=男性主导」两组同义替换都成立，C 是定位句的精确同义\n复述。\nD) 无中生有\n全文没有任何句子谈及编辑的「相关背景／资质」是否充足；P2 谈的是性别和国籍分\n布，不是专业相关性，have relevant backgrounds 凭空冒出。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What fact does the author highlight concerning the gender differences in editors of psychology journals?", "options": ["A) There were quite a few female editors who also distinguished themselves as influential psychology researchers.", "B) The number of female editors was simply disproportionate to that of women engaged in psychology research.", "C) The proportion of female editors was increasingly lower at senior levels.", "D) There were few female editors who could move up to senior positions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What fact does the author highlight concerning the gender differences in editors of\npsychology journals? 问的是「作者强调了什么事实」，原文有一句直接陈述，属细节而非\n主旨。\n【拆句】题干主干 What fact does the author highlight…?；concerning the gender differences\nin editors of psychology journals 是 concerning 引导的介词短语作 highlight 的范围状\n语，限定话题。结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】Crucially, the proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion\nof women psychology researchers.\n至关重要的是，女性编辑的占比显著低于心理学领域整体女性研究者的占比。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只对比「女编辑占比」与「女研究者占比」，并未说有「兼任出色研究者的女编\n辑」；distinguished themselves as influential psychology researchers 是凭空添加\n的属性。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe proportion of female editors was significantly lower than the overall proportion of women\npsychology researchers\n↔\nThe number of female editors was simply disproportionate to that of women engaged in\npsychology research.\nthe overall proportion of women psychology researchers ↔\nthat of women engaged in psychology research\n唯一刻画「女编辑占比 vs 女研究者占比」这一比例对照的选项是 B；其它选项要么换了\n对比对象，要么换了讨论范围。\nC) 过度推断\nThe proportion of female editors was increasingly lower at senior levels. 把原文\n「各级别男编辑都更多」的事实曲解为「越往高级越少」的递进趋势；原文 There were\nsignificantly more male than female editors at each level of seniority 只说「每一\n级都更多」，并没有给出「越往上越严重」的递进关系。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文没有讨论「女编辑能否升迁」这一职业流动问题；few female editors who could\nmove up to senior positions 把性别比例话题换成升迁话题，方向错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What can we infer from the conclusion drawn by the team of the new study on the basis of their findings?", "options": ["A) Women's views are underrepresented in the editorial boards of top psychology and neuroscience journals.", "B) Male editors of top psychology and neuroscience journals tend to be biased against their female colleagues.", "C) Male researchers have enough representation in the editorial boards to ensure their publications.", "D) Female editors have to struggle to get women's research articles published in academic journals."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What can we infer from the conclusion drawn by the team of the new study on the\nbasis of their findings? 含明确的 infer 标志词，正确答案是定位句的一步同义延伸，而非\n原文逐字表述。\n【拆句】题干主干 What can we infer from the conclusion…?；drawn by the team of the new\nstudy 是过去分词短语作后置定语，修饰 conclusion；on the basis of their findings 是介\n词短语状语，说明结论的依据。三层修饰仅在定位 conclusion 这个核心名词，不影响主干\n理解，但基础薄弱的同学要看清 from 后面真正的对象是 conclusion 而不是 team。\n【定位】Based on their results, the team concludes that \"the ideas, values and decision-making biases\nof men are overrepresented in the editorial positions of the most recognized academic\njournals in psychology and neuroscience.\"\n基于研究结果，研究团队总结道：「在最受认可的心理学与神经科学学术期刊的编辑岗位\n上，男性的想法、价值观与决策偏见被过度代表。」\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe ideas, values and decision-making biases of men are overrepresented ↔\nWomen's views are underrepresented in the editorial boards of top psychology and\nneuroscience journals.\nthe editorial positions of the most recognized academic journals in psychology and neuroscience\n↔ the editorial boards of top psychology and neuroscience journals\n在「男过度代表」基础上仅走一步就到「女代表不足」，A 是唯一这样做的；其它选项\n要么走第二步（偏见、歧视），要么换了话题。\nB) 过度推断\n原文 decision-making biases of men 指的是「决策中的偏见模式被过度呈现」这一统\n计事实；be biased against their female colleagues 则把它推到「男编辑刻意歧视女\n同事」的人际行为层面，多走了一步。\nC) 答非所问\n题干问「能推断出什么」，ensure their publications 谈的是「男编辑的发表得到保\n障」，原文没有任何句子谈论这种保障机制；且本研究的论调是批评不平等而非赞颂男\n性发表优势，与原文立场相反。\nD) 无中生有\n原文从未谈论女编辑为发表女性研究而「斗争」，struggle to get women's research\narticles published 是凭空虚构出来的情节。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest we do instead of simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition?", "options": ["A) Strike a balance between male and female editors.", "B) Increase women's employment in senior positions.", "C) Enlarge the body of female academics.", "D) Implement overall structural reforms."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest we do instead of simply blaming the inequality of\neditorial boards on tradition? 问作者明确建议什么，原文有 Rather than… we should…\n的直陈句，是细节题而非主旨题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author suggest we do，what 作 do 的宾语前置；instead of\nsimply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition 是 instead of 引导的动\n名词介词短语，作状语，表达「与其…不如…」的对比关系。看清 instead of 后面是「要被\n否定的旧做法」，suggest we do 后面才是「要肯定的新做法」，二者不可弄反。\n【定位】Instead, there are structural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science. Women receive\nlower salaries and face greater childcare demands, for instance, which can result in fewer\npublications and grants—the kinds of things that journals look for when deciding who to\nappoint. Rather than simply blaming the inequality of editorial boards on tradition, we should\nbe actively breaking down these existing barriers.\n实际上，是结构性的原因让女性在科学界处于不利地位。例如，女性薪资更低、育儿负担更\n重，这会导致她们论文与基金更少——而这些恰恰是期刊在选拔编辑时所看重的。与其简单\n地把编委会的不平等推给传统，不如主动去拆除这些既有的壁垒。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nStrike a balance between male and female editors 只触及「编辑性别比例」这一表\n层结果，与作者主张的「拆除导致不平等的结构性原因」相比，停留在症状而非根因；\n原文反对的就是「只盯比例不动结构」的浅层对策。\nB) 答非所问\nIncrease women's employment in senior positions 借的是 P6 「初级岗位也男性偏\n多」这一对照性数据；但 P6 是作者用来「驳斥传统论」的论据，并非建议；55 题问的\n是「P7 作者建议什么」。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 This implies that a lack of female academics is not the problem. 已明确否定\n「女学者太少」是问题；Enlarge the body of female academics 恰恰反过来主张「扩\n充女学者队伍」，方向完全相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nactively breaking down these existing barriers ↔ Implement overall structural reforms.\nstructural reasons that women are disadvantaged in science ↔ structural reforms\n把「主动拆除结构性壁垒」概括为「实施整体结构性改革」，是 D 与原文唯一吻合的\n同义改写；其它选项要么停留在「男女比例」「升迁」等局部对策，要么误把原因当\n对策。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
